var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"Auto-generated from docstrings, one page per area. See Conventions for the numerical and physical conventions behind these functions.\n\nData types and I/O: TimeSeries, FrequencySeries and Spectrogram, GWOSC and frame-file I/O, the download cache, and published posterior samples.\nTime and segments: GPS/UTC conversion with leap seconds, Segment/SegmentList algebra, and Greenwich mean sidereal time.\nSpectral analysis: PSD/ASD and cross-spectral estimation, coherence, spectrograms and the Q-transform.\nFiltering and resampling: Butterworth and notch filters, whitening, and polyphase resampling.\nDetectors and reference curves: detector geometry, antenna response, arrival-time delays, and the aLIGO/Advanced Virgo design-sensitivity curves.","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/09_coherence/#Coherence-and-CSD","page":"Coherence & CSD","text":"The coherence between two channels is a frequency-domain measure of how correlated they are: at each frequency bin it's a number in [0, 1], 0 meaning \"no linear relationship whatsoever\" and 1 meaning \"one channel is an exact (possibly scaled/delayed) copy of the other at that frequency\". This tutorial uses one classic application of coherence in GW data analysis: comparing the two strain channels from independent, geographically separated detectors (LIGO-Hanford \"H1\" and LIGO-Livingston \"L1\") around GW150914. Real astrophysical strain coherence between two sites is far too weak to be statistically visible this way, so this is really a null-hypothesis check — and, as we'll see, the result (near-zero broadband coherence with a couple of narrow, physically-explicable spikes) is itself the interesting, correct answer, not a bug.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Coherence & CSD"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/09_coherence/#Fetching-two-detector-data","page":"Coherence & CSD","text":"We use the standard 4096 s H1 and L1 segments around GW150914 shared by these tutorials (the same data csd/coherence are validated on; see the Conventions page).\n\nh1_path = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126260736)\nl1_path = fetch_open_data(\"L1\", 1126256640, 1126260736)\nh1_full, _ = load_gwosc_strain(h1_path)\nl1_full, _ = load_gwosc_strain(l1_path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\ncsd/coherence require the two TimeSeries to share an identical sample grid (same dt, same length) with no missing data. GWOSC's public files pad any non-science-mode stretch with NaN, and this particular L1 file happens to have one such gap in its first ~190 seconds (well before GW150914 itself) — so we crop both series down to a clean, off-source 1024 s window, safely clear of both that gap and the merger itself:\n\ntrigger_gps = 1126259462.4\nwindow_end = trigger_gps - 2.0\nwindow_start = window_end - 1024.0\n\nh1 = crop(h1_full, window_start, window_end)\nl1 = crop(l1_full, window_start, window_end)\n\ncount(isnan, h1.data), count(isnan, l1.data)\n(0, 0)","title":"Fetching two-detector data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/09_coherence/#Cross-spectral-density-(CSD)","page":"Coherence & CSD","text":"csd is the complex-valued generalization of psd to a pair of channels: Welch-averaged (Hann-windowed, 50%-overlapping segments, mean- not median-averaged since a median of complex values isn't a well-defined robust statistic — see docs/CONVENTIONS.md). coherence is built directly on top of it (|Pxy|^2 / (Pxx * Pyy)), so it's worth looking at on its own first. seglen is in samples, matching psd/asd's own convention — a 4-second segment at H1/L1's shared 4096 Hz sample rate is 4 * 4096 samples:\n\nseglen = 4 * 4096\npxy = csd(h1, l1; seglen=seglen, overlap=0.5)\nFrequencySeries{ComplexF64}(8193 bins, f0=0.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain---strain CSD\")\n\nThe default FrequencySeries recipe is log-log, so (as with the PSD/ASD tutorial's reference-curve plots) we first crop away the DC bin – f=0 can't be shown on a log-frequency axis – and restrict to the 20-1024 Hz band:\n\nplot_band = (20.0, 1024.0)\npxy_band = crop(pxy, plot_band...)\nFrequencySeries{ComplexF64}(4016 bins, f0=20.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain---strain CSD\")\n\nplot(pxy_band; ylabel=\"|CSD| (strain^2 / Hz)\", title=\"H1-L1 cross-spectral density magnitude\",\n     label=\"|CSD(H1, L1)|\", color=:purple)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\ncsd returns a complex FrequencySeries; the default plot recipe already plots its magnitude on log-log axes (the same convention as a PSD/ASD plot, since |Pxy(f)| has the same units and dynamic range as Pxx(f)/Pyy(f)). Like the two detectors' individual noise spectra, it spans many orders of magnitude and has broadband structure — but magnitude alone doesn't tell us how much of that is genuine cross-correlation versus each channel's own individual noise level. That normalized answer is exactly what coherence gives us.","title":"Cross-spectral density (CSD)"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/09_coherence/#Coherence","page":"Coherence & CSD","text":"coherence normalizes csd by each channel's own PSD (Pxx, Pyy, both mean-Welch via psd(...; method=:mean) for consistency with Pxy's own averaging), giving a real-valued result in [0, 1] at every frequency bin — how much of each channel's power at that frequency is linearly explained by the other:\n\ncoh = coherence(h1, l1; seglen=seglen, overlap=0.5)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(8193 bins, f0=0.0, df=0.25, name=\"Coherence between strain and strain\")\n\nWe plot with a log-scaled frequency axis and a linear coherence axis fixed to [0, 1]: the FrequencySeries recipe defaults to log-log axes (appropriate for PSD/ASD/CSD-magnitude curves), so for this linearly-scaled [0, 1] quantity we override yscale explicitly – and, as above, crop away the unplottable f=0 DC bin first.\n\ncoh_band = crop(coh, plot_band...)\nplot(coh_band; xlabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", ylabel=\"Coherence\", yscale=:identity, ylim=(0, 1),\n     label=\"Coherence(H1, L1)\", color=:black,\n     title=\"H1-L1 coherence (GW150914 off-source window)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Coherence"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/09_coherence/#What-this-actually-shows","page":"Coherence & CSD","text":"H1 and L1 are ~3000 km apart with independent seismic/electronic/optical noise sources, so there is no physical reason for their noise to be correlated at any frequency — and no astrophysical signal in this off-source window either. The plot above bears that out: coherence sits at a low, broadband floor essentially everywhere, in the 20-1024 Hz band it measures (computed directly from coh, not eyeballed off the plot):\n\nband_freqs = collect(frequencies(coh_band))\nmean_coh = sum(coh_band.data) / length(coh_band)\nmedian_coh = sort(coh_band.data)[length(coh_band) ÷ 2]\nmean_coh, median_coh\n(0.002033746972736798, 0.001416250388601769)\n\nThat floor is not exactly zero, and shouldn't be: with seglen/overlap fixed by the window above, this estimate averages over N = 511 Welch segments, and even two genuinely independent, uncorrelated Gaussian-noise channels have an expected coherence estimate of about 1/N at every bin (a well-known small-sample bias of the coherence estimator) rather than 0 — 1/511 ≈ 0.00196, right in line with the ~0.002 mean measured here. Averaging over more segments (a longer window, or a shorter seglen at fixed window length) pushes this floor down further; it never reaches exactly zero for a finite amount of data.\n\n(N follows directly from the segmentation rule shared by psd/csd/coherence — seglen-sample segments starting round(seglen * (1 - overlap)) samples apart, see docs/CONVENTIONS.md — so it's reproducible from length(h1), seglen, and overlap alone without reaching into any internal helper.)\n\nstep = round(Int, seglen * (1 - 0.5))\nn_segments = fld(length(h1) - seglen, step) + 1\nn_segments, 1 / n_segments\n(511, 0.0019569471624266144)\n\nA handful of narrow bins do rise well above that floor — the strongest are visible as small spikes in the plot around 60 Hz and 120 Hz:\n\nidx_sorted = sortperm(coh_band.data; rev=true)\n[(band_freqs[k], coh_band.data[k]) for k in idx_sorted[1:5]]\n5-element Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}:\n (39.5, 0.02363984301157723)\n (853.25, 0.017539503039949306)\n (59.75, 0.016288093399612985)\n (120.0, 0.014961069545202424)\n (911.25, 0.014437682537733938)\n\n60 Hz (and its second harmonic, 120 Hz) is North American AC mains frequency. Even though H1 and L1 are on separate power grids hundreds of kilometers apart, mains-frequency electronics noise and the associated harmonics are a famously common shared line in both LIGO sites' instrumental noise — the classic example of coherence analysis is exactly this kind of coupling within one detector, between its strain channel and its mains-power monitor. Seeing a faint version of the same line show up between two independent detectors here is a real, physically sensible artifact of shared power-grid-frequency instrumental behavior — not evidence of an astrophysical signal. Crucially, even these peaks reach only a few percent coherence, nowhere near the 1.0 a truly common signal (or a single-segment estimate, which is trivially 1.0 everywhere — see coherence's own docstring for why averaging over multiple segments is essential) would produce. That combination — near-zero broadband floor consistent with the 1/N bias, plus small but non-zero peaks at known shared instrumental lines — is exactly the expected, correct result for two independent detectors' noise, and is itself a standard data-quality diagnostic: an unexpectedly large or broadband H1-L1 coherence would be the actual red flag, hinting at some shared environmental or electronics coupling between sites that has no business being there.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this actually shows"},{"category":"section","location":"api/spectral/#Spectral-analysis","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"PSD/ASD and cross-spectral estimation, coherence, spectrograms and the Q-transform.","title":"Spectral analysis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.PSD._welch_starts-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"_welch_starts(n::Integer, seglen::Integer, overlap::Real) -> AbstractRange{Int}\n\n1-based starting indices of Welch segments of length seglen with fractional overlap, covering a series of length n. Factored out of psd so Coherence.jl's csd/coherence use the exact same segmentation convention (same step size, same \"shorter than one segment\" error) rather than a hand-mirrored copy that could silently drift from this one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.PSD._welch_starts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.PSD._welch_window-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"_welch_window(seglen::Integer) -> (win, win_power)\n\nThe Hann window (DSP.Windows.hanning) used for every Welch-segmented estimate in this package (psd/asd here, csd/coherence in Coherence.jl), plus its power-loss correction factor mean(win.^2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.PSD._welch_window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.PSD.asd-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"asd(ts::TimeSeries; kwargs...) -> FrequencySeries\n\nAmplitude spectral density, sqrt.(psd(ts; kwargs...)) – same Welch/median convention as psd (see its docstring), just square-rooted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.PSD.asd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.PSD.psd-Union{Tuple{TimeSeries{T, V} where V<:AbstractVector{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T<:Real","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"psd(ts::TimeSeries; seglen::Int, overlap::Real=0.5, method::Symbol=:median) -> FrequencySeries\n\nWelch-method one-sided power spectral density estimate over a (typically long, off-source) TimeSeries: split into seglen-sample segments with fractional overlap, Hann-window each (DSP.Windows.hanning, also scipy.signal.welch's default). This is deliberately a different window from condition_segment's Tukey taper on the actual analysis segment – that one exists to suppress edge discontinuities in a single non-periodic segment being FFT'd once, not to run a Welch average.\n\nmethod=:median (default, the standard LIGO convention) uses the per-frequency-bin median across segments rather than the mean, which is far more robust to loud transient glitches contaminating a handful of segments; debiased by the exponential distribution's ln(2) median correction (asymptotically exact for the segment counts here – O(500) segments for a 1024s off-source stretch at 4s/50% overlap – see e.g. Allen et al. 2005 for the finite-sample correction this approximates). method=:mean skips both the median and the bias correction.\n\nNormalization is the standard density convention (scipy.signal.welch's scaling=\"density\"): ∫psd(f) df over [0, Nyquist] approximates the time series' variance.\n\nReturns a FrequencySeries with f0=0.0, df=1/(seglen*dt), real-valued data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.PSD.psd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Coherence.coherence-Union{Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{TimeSeries{T1, V} where V<:AbstractVector{T1}, TimeSeries{T2, V} where V<:AbstractVector{T2}}} where {T1<:Real, T2<:Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"coherence(ts1::TimeSeries, ts2::TimeSeries; seglen::Int, overlap::Real=0.5) -> FrequencySeries\n\nMagnitude-squared (Welch-averaged) coherence between two TimeSeries: |Pxy(f)|^2 / (Pxx(f) * Pyy(f)), real-valued, in [0, 1]. Follows scipy.signal.coherence; see this module's header comment for the normalization/averaging conventions.\n\nRequires averaging over multiple segments to be meaningful: a single-segment estimate is trivially 1.0 at every frequency (Pxy, Pxx, Pyy all computed from the same one FFT), which is exactly why csd/coherence always mean-average over seglen-sample, overlap-fraction-overlapping segments rather than accepting seglen == length(ts).\n\nPxx/Pyy are computed via psd(ts; seglen, overlap, method=:mean) (mean-averaged, matching scipy.signal.coherence's own average=\"mean\" default and keeping Pxy's numerator and Pxx/ Pyy's denominator on the exact same averaging convention); Pxy via csd. Requires matching dt/length, same as csd.\n\nReturns a FrequencySeries{Float64} with f0=0.0, df=1/(seglen*dt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Coherence.coherence"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Coherence.csd-Union{Tuple{T2}, Tuple{T1}, Tuple{TimeSeries{T1, V} where V<:AbstractVector{T1}, TimeSeries{T2, V} where V<:AbstractVector{T2}}} where {T1<:Real, T2<:Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"csd(ts1::TimeSeries, ts2::TimeSeries; seglen::Int, overlap::Real=0.5) -> FrequencySeries\n\nWelch-method one-sided cross-spectral density estimate between two TimeSeries sampled on the same grid (same dt; t0 need not match – only the bin-by-bin sample correspondence used for each FFT segment matters, matching scipy.signal.csd's own lack of an epoch concept). See this module's header comment for the normalization/averaging conventions.\n\nSegments as in psd (same _welch_starts/_welch_window helpers: seglen-sample segments, fractional overlap, Hann window), mean-averaged (not median). Complex-valued: Pxy(f) = mean_over_segments(conj(rfft(x)) .* rfft(y)) * scale, one-sided density normalization (scipy.signal.welch's scaling=\"density\").\n\nReturns a FrequencySeries{ComplexF64} with f0=0.0, df=1/(seglen*dt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Coherence.csd"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Spectrograms.Spectrogram","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"Spectrogram{T,M<:AbstractMatrix{T}}\n\nA uniformly-sampled time-frequency series: data is an nfreq x ntime matrix (row i, column j is the power in frequency bin i during time bin j – the same (freq, time) axis order DSP.spectrogram uses for its power field). Row i is at frequency f0 + (i-1) * df (Hz); column j is centered at GPS time t0 + (j-1) * dt (seconds) – i.e. t0 here is the GPS time of the center of the first STFT segment, not the start of the input TimeSeries, matching how DSP.spectrogram's own time field is defined (segment-center times).\n\nSpectrogram(data, t0, dt, f0, df; name=nothing)\n\nname is optional free-text metadata, as with TimeSeries/FrequencySeries.\n\nUse the existing times/frequencies generic functions (this module adds methods to the ones Types.jl exports) to get the GPS time axis / frequency axis as ranges, exactly as for TimeSeries/FrequencySeries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Spectrograms.Spectrogram"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#Base.getindex-Tuple{Spectrogram, Colon, Integer}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"s[:, j] -> FrequencySeries\n\nThe jth time bin's power spectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#Base.getindex-Tuple{Spectrogram, Integer, Colon}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"s[i, :] -> TimeSeries\n\nThe ith frequency bin's power evolution over time (a \"time series of one frequency bin\", uniformly sampled at the spectrogram's time-bin spacing dt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.getindex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Spectrograms.spectrogram-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"spectrogram(ts::TimeSeries; n=length(ts) >> 3, noverlap=n >> 1, nfft=DSP.nextfastfft(n),\n            window=DSP.Windows.hanning, onesided=eltype(ts) <: Real,\n            name=ts.name) -> Spectrogram\n\nShort-time Fourier transform (STFT) power spectrogram of ts: n-sample segments, noverlap samples of overlap between consecutive segments, each segment windowed and FFT'd independently (no averaging across segments within a column – see the module-level note above for how this differs from a Welch/median-averaged spectrogram). Thin wrapper around DSP.spectrogram.\n\nDefaults match DSP.spectrogram's own (n = length(ts) >> 3, 50% overlap, nfft = DSP.nextfastfft(n), window = DSP.Windows.hanning, i.e. zerophase=false). This is the standard spectral-analysis convention (window peak centered in the middle of the segment, as scipy.signal.spectrogram also uses). It is tempting to reach for DSP.Windows.hanning(n; zerophase=true) for this, since scipy's own docs call its own convention \"periodic\" – but DSP.jl's zerophase=true means something else entirely: per that function's own docstring, it centers the window's peak at index 1 with the negative half wrapped to the end (a zero-phase-filter-kernel arrangement in the time domain, not a windowing-for-spectral-analysis arrangement) – using it here silently redistributes power across frequency bins by up to several orders of magnitude at some frequencies.\n\nThe returned Spectrogram's GPS time axis is derived from ts.t0: DSP.spectrogram's own time field gives segment-center times in seconds relative to the start of the input signal, so Spectrogram.t0 = ts.t0 + (that first segment-center offset).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Spectrograms.spectrogram"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.frequencies-Tuple{Spectrogram}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"frequencies(s::Spectrogram) -> AbstractRange{Float64}\n\nFrequency (Hz) of every frequency bin (row): f0 .+ df .* (0:nfreq-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.frequencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.times-Tuple{Spectrogram}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"times(s::Spectrogram) -> AbstractRange{Float64}\n\nGPS time of the center of every time bin (column): t0 .+ dt .* (0:ntime-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.times"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QPeak","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"QPeak(time, frequency, energy, snr, q)\n\nThe loudest tile of a QScan (or the loudest across a multi-Q search): its GPS time, frequency (Hz), energy (normalized, e.g. by median if norm=:median), snr = sqrt(2*energy), and the q of the plane it came from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QPeak"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QRow","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"QRow(frequency, energy, t0, dt)\n\nOne frequency row's (normalized) energy time series from a Q-plane transform: energy[k] is the energy at GPS time t0 + (k-1)*dt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QRow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QScan","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"QScan(q, frange, rows)\n\nThe full energy grid for one Q-plane: rows (one per tile frequency), each with its own time resolution (rows near higher frequencies get finer time resolution). No re-gridding onto a common time/frequency axis is done; interpolate the rows yourself for display.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QScan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QTileParams","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"QTileParams(q, frequency, duration, sampling, mismatch)\n\nParameters of a single Q-transform tile row: a fixed Q, central frequency (Hz), analysis segment duration (s) and sampling rate (Hz), and tiling mismatch. The tile computations are the free functions below (qtile_window, qtile_data_indices, qtile_padding, qtile_energy).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.QTileParams"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.energy_series-Tuple{QRow}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"energy_series(row::QRow) -> TimeSeries\n\nWrap a QRow's energy vector as a TimeSeries for times/crop/etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.energy_series"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.normalized_fft-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"normalized_fft(ts::TimeSeries) -> FrequencySeries{ComplexF64}\n\nOne-sided, amplitude-normalized FFT: X = rfft(data) / N, then every bin except DC is doubled. This (not a bare rfft) is the FrequencySeries convention the rest of this module's tile computations assume; the per-tile normalization (qtile_window) is calibrated against it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.normalized_fft"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.plane_frange-Tuple{Real, Real, Tuple{Real, Real}, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"plane_frange(q, dur, frange, fs) -> (fmin, fmax)\n\nSingle-Q-plane frange resolution (only relevant when a plane is used standalone with an unresolved frange, e.g. (0, Inf) – q_scan/q_transform instead resolve frange once via tiling_frange and reuse it across every Q).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.plane_frange"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.plane_frequencies-Tuple{Real, Tuple{Real, Real}, Real, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"plane_frequencies(q, frange, dur, mismatch) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCentral frequencies of the tile rows in a single Q-plane spanning frange = (fmin, fmax), log-spaced densely enough for mismatch, each snapped down to the nearest multiple of 1/dur (the frequency-bin spacing of a dur-second segment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.plane_frequencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_plane_transform-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Real, Tuple{Real, Real}, Real, Real, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"q_plane_transform(fseries_data, q, frange, dur, fs, epoch; mismatch=0.2, norm=:median) -> QScan\n\nCompute the full energy grid for a single Q-plane: one qtile_energy row per frequency from plane_frequencies, normalized by norm (:median, :mean, or :none).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_plane_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_scan-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, Real, Real, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"q_scan(fseries_data, dur, fs, epoch; qrange=(4,64), frange=(0,Inf), mismatch=0.2, norm=:median,\n       search=nothing) -> (QScan, QPeak)\n\nMulti-Q search: compute a q_plane_transform at each of q_values(qrange, dur, mismatch), and return the plane containing the single loudest tile (and that tile's QPeak). The most significant Q is chosen this way rather than by merging energies across Q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_scan"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_transform-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"q_transform(ts::TimeSeries; qrange=(4,64), frange=(0,Inf), mismatch=0.2, norm=:median,\n            search=nothing) -> (QScan, QPeak)\n\nCompute the constant-Q transform of ts (via normalized_fft; the input is not whitened, so call whiten first if needed) and return the loudest Q-plane's full energy grid plus its peak tile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_transform"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_values-Tuple{Tuple{Real, Real}, Real, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"q_values(qrange, dur, mismatch) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nLogarithmically-spaced Q values spanning qrange = (qlow, qhigh), dense enough that neighbouring planes' tiles mismatch by no more than mismatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.q_values"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qscan_peak-Tuple{QScan}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"qscan_peak(scan::QScan; search=nothing) -> QPeak\n\nFind the loudest tile in scan (the maximum-energy sample across every row), optionally restricted to a search = (t_lo, t_hi) GPS time window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qscan_peak"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_data_indices-Tuple{QTileParams}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"qtile_data_indices(t::QTileParams) -> UnitRange-like Vector{Int}\n\n1-based Julia indices into a normalized_fft-style one-sided FrequencySeries selecting the frequency bins this tile's window applies to: round(offset + 1 + frequency*duration) + 1. The final +1 converts 0-based to 1-based indexing; the inner +1 is an offset inherited from the original Omega pipeline, kept as-is so results match other Omega-derived implementations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_data_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_energy-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Complex}, QTileParams}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"qtile_energy(fseries_data::AbstractVector{<:Complex}, t::QTileParams) -> (energy, dt)\n\nUn-normalized-by-median/mean tile energy: window+select the relevant bins of fseries_data (a normalized_fft-convention one-sided spectrum), zero-pad, ifftshift, inverse-FFT, and take abs2. Returns the energy vector and its sample spacing dt = duration/ntiles. The median/mean normalization is applied afterwards by q_plane_transform.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_energy"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_padding-Tuple{QTileParams}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"qtile_padding(t::QTileParams) -> (left, right)\n\nZero-padding (in bins) applied to a tile's windowed frequency-domain data before the IFFT, so the padded length equals _ntiles(t) (a power of 2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_padding"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_window-Tuple{QTileParams}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"qtile_window(t::QTileParams) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe \"bi-square\" frequency-domain window (1 - x^2)^2, normalized so the tile's IFFT energy has the right absolute scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.qtile_window"},{"category":"method","location":"api/spectral/#GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.tiling_frange-Tuple{Tuple{Real, Real}, Tuple{Real, Real}, Real, Real, Real}","page":"Spectral Analysis","text":"tiling_frange(qrange, frange, dur, fs, mismatch) -> (fmin, fmax)\n\nResolve a (fmin, fmax) frequency range for a multi-Q scan: fmin == 0 is replaced by 50*max(q)/(2*pi*dur), fmax == Inf (or too high for the Q range) is replaced/clamped to fs/2/(1+sqrt(11)/min(q)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.QTransform.tiling_frange"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#Plotting-Recipes","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"GravitationalWaveData.jl's plotting support uses RecipesBase.jl's @recipe macro, defined once per type in ext/GravitationalWaveDataRecipesBaseExt.jl. A @recipe function plot(ts::TimeSeries) block is really just a method of RecipesBase.apply_recipe reached via multiple dispatch — when a RecipesBase-backed plotting package (Plots.jl here) sees plot(ts) and ts isn't already a type it knows how to draw, it asks RecipesBase whether any recipe applies, and ours fires. The recipe's job is just to (1) set some default plot attributes and (2) hand back the (x, y) (or (x, y, z)) data to actually plot — nothing about TimeSeries/FrequencySeries/Spectrogram needs to know about plotting at all. That's also why this only works once a plotting package is loaded: GravitationalWaveData itself has no hard dependency on Plots.jl, ext/GravitationalWaveDataRecipesBaseExt.jl is a package extension that only compiles once both GravitationalWaveData and RecipesBase are present in the session (see that file's own header comment).\n\nThis tutorial works directly from ext/GravitationalWaveDataRecipesBaseExt.jl's source: what each recipe's defaults are, how to override them, and how they compose with the rest of Plots.jl (multi-panel layouts, plain Plots.jl primitives like vline!).\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Plotting Recipes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#Fetching-real-data","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"As with the other tutorials, we reuse this project's standard GW150914 H1 cross-check segment (already locally cached from the Getting Started / PSD & ASD / Coherence tutorials, so this is free after the first fetch anywhere in the docs build):\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126260736)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\nmerger_gps = 1126259462.4\n1.1262594624e9\n\nA ten-second zoom around the merger for the TimeSeries examples (raw strain is noise-dominated at full 4096 s scale, so a zoom is more legible on a line plot):\n\nzoom = crop(strain, merger_gps - 5, merger_gps + 5)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(40960 samples, t0=1.1262594573999023e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=10.0s, name=\"strain\")\n\nAn asd of the full segment for the FrequencySeries examples, cropped to a plottable band (log-log axes can't show the f=0 DC bin, and the aLIGO band of interest is 10-2000 Hz anyway — same band the PSD & ASD tutorial uses):\n\nh1_asd = asd(strain; seglen=4 * 4096, overlap=0.5)\nasd_band = crop(h1_asd, 10.0, 2000.0)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(7960 bins, f0=10.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain ASD\")\n\nA spectrogram of a 16 s window around the merger for the Spectrogram examples (the full 4096 s segment would make for a very wide, slow-to-render heatmap; 16 s is enough to see structure without the cost):\n\nspec_zoom = crop(strain, merger_gps - 8, merger_gps + 8)\nspec = spectrogram(spec_zoom)\nSpectrogram{Float64}(4097 freq bins x 15 time bins, t0=1.1262594553999023e9, dt=1.0, f0=0.0, df=0.5, name=\"strain\")","title":"Fetching real data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#1.-Plain-defaults","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"With no keyword arguments at all, each recipe's own defaults apply. Reading ext/GravitationalWaveDataRecipesBaseExt.jl:\n\nTimeSeries: seriestype --> :path, linear-linear axes, xlabel --> \"GPS Time (s)\", ylabel --> \"Amplitude\", label taken from ts.name if set.\nFrequencySeries: seriestype --> :path, but xscale --> :log10 and yscale --> :log10 — the near-universal convention for PSD/ASD-shaped curves in GW data analysis.\nSpectrogram: seriestype --> :heatmap, x = GPS time, y = frequency, color = power, with colorbar_scale --> :log10 since raw STFT power spans many orders of magnitude.\n\nplot(zoom)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nplot(asd_band)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nplot(spec)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"1. Plain defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#2.-Overriding-recipe-defaults","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"Every attribute in every recipe above is set with -->, never :=. In RecipesBase, --> means \"use this value unless the caller already supplied one for this same keyword in this same call\" — it's a default, not a fixed choice. := (which none of these three recipes use) would instead force the value regardless of what the caller passes, which is exactly why none of xlabel, ylabel, seriestype, xscale/yscale, or colorbar_scale is fixed with it: nothing about a TimeSeries/FrequencySeries/Spectrogram's own correctness depends on a particular color, label, or even (for FrequencySeries) a log-scaled axis — a caller with a linearly-scaled quantity stored in a FrequencySeries (like coherence, see the Coherence & CSD tutorial) needs to be able to opt out of the log-log default, and --> is exactly what makes plot(fs; yscale=:identity) work.\n\nConcretely, every one of these keywords passed explicitly simply wins over the recipe's own --> default in the same call:\n\nplot(zoom; color=:firebrick, linewidth=2, title=\"H1 strain around GW150914\",\n     ylabel=\"Strain\", label=\"H1\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nseriestype itself is just another attribute set with -->, so it's overridable the same way — nothing pins a TimeSeries to a line plot specifically:\n\nplot(zoom; seriestype=:scatter, markersize=1.5, markerstrokewidth=0, color=:darkorange,\n     title=\"H1 strain around GW150914 (scatter override)\", ylabel=\"Strain\", label=\"H1\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nplot(asd_band; color=:navy, linewidth=1.5, title=\"H1 measured ASD\",\n     ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\", label=\"H1 ASD\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nplot(spec; color=:plasma, colorbar_scale=:identity,\n     title=\"H1 spectrogram around GW150914 (linear color scale, plasma)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"2. Overriding recipe defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#The-plot!()-overlay-gotcha","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"--> only avoids clobbering an explicit keyword within the call it's evaluated in — it does not remember an attribute set by an earlier call on the same plot object. Building a plot incrementally with plot!() re-triggers the recipe (and its --> defaults) on every call, so an attribute you set explicitly on the first call is silently reset back to the recipe's own default on the second unless you repeat it. Concretely, overlaying the same FrequencySeries recipe twice with only the first call's ylabel set:\n\np_bad = plot(asd_band; ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\", label=\"full band\", color=:navy)\nplot!(p_bad, crop(asd_band, 100.0, 300.0); label=\"100-300 Hz zoom\", color=:orange)\np_bad.subplots[1][:yaxis][:guide]   # reset back to the FrequencySeries recipe's own \"Amplitude\" default\n\"Amplitude\"\n\nPassing ylabel explicitly on every call fixes it:\n\np_good = plot(asd_band; ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\", label=\"full band\", color=:navy)\nplot!(p_good, crop(asd_band, 100.0, 300.0); ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\",\n      label=\"100-300 Hz zoom\", color=:orange)\np_good.subplots[1][:yaxis][:guide]\n\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\"","title":"The plot!() overlay gotcha"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#3.-Multi-panel-layouts","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"GravitationalWaveData.jl's recipes hand back plain (x, y)/(x, y, z) data plus attribute defaults, so multi-panel figures are exactly ordinary Plots.jl layout= composition — no special support needed on the recipe side. Building each panel as its own Plot object first (each ;-suppressed so it isn't displayed on its own — see the note below), then combining with layout=:\n\np_time = plot(zoom; color=:black, ylabel=\"Strain\", title=\"H1 strain (time domain)\", label=\"H1\");\np_freq = plot(asd_band; color=:navy, ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\",\n              title=\"H1 ASD (frequency domain)\", label=\"H1\");\n\n(Both plot(...) calls above end with ;, suppressing that cell's own display — otherwise, per the Documenter @example-block duplicate-render trap, each panel would render once here on its own and again inside the combined figure below.)\n\nplot(p_time, p_freq; layout=(2, 1), size=(700, 600))\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nA third panel (the spectrogram) composes the same way — layout=(3, 1) stacks all three, time domain, frequency domain, and time-frequency, for the same 16 s window used above:\n\np_spec = plot(spec; title=\"H1 spectrogram (time-frequency domain)\");\nplot(plot(spec_zoom; color=:black, ylabel=\"Strain\", title=\"H1 strain (time domain)\", label=\"H1\"),\n     p_freq, p_spec; layout=(3, 1), size=(700, 850))\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}","title":"3. Multi-panel layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/11_plotting_recipes/#4.-Composing-with-plain-Plots.jl-primitives","page":"Plotting Recipes","text":"Because a GravitationalWaveData.jl recipe plot is an ordinary Plots.jl Plot object once built, everything else in the Plots.jl API — vline!, hline!, annotate!, scatter!, and so on — works on it completely normally. The recipe system is not a closed box: it only ever runs once, at the initial plot(...)/plot!(...) call for a GravitationalWaveData.jl series; anything added afterward is just standard Plots.jl.\n\nMarking the GW150914 merger time on the time-domain zoom plot:\n\np_marked = plot(zoom; color=:black, ylabel=\"Strain\", label=\"H1\",\n                title=\"H1 strain around GW150914 (merger marked)\")\nvline!(p_marked, [merger_gps]; color=:red, linestyle=:dash, linewidth=1.5, label=\"merger\")\nhline!(p_marked, [0.0]; color=:gray, linestyle=:dot, linewidth=1, label=false)\nannotate!(p_marked, merger_gps, maximum(zoom.data) * 0.9,\n          Plots.text(\"GW150914\", 9, :red, :left))\np_marked\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}\n\nNone of vline!, hline!, or annotate! know or care that zoom came from a @recipe rather than a hand-built Plots.jl series — the recipe's only job was translating a TimeSeries into (x, y) data and some default attributes once, at the initial plot() call; every subsequent Plots.jl call composes with the result exactly as it would with any other plot.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"4. Composing with plain Plots.jl primitives"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#Spectrogram","page":"Spectrogram","text":"A spectrogram shows how a signal's frequency content evolves over time: a sequence of short-time Fourier transforms (STFTs), one per time bin, stacked side by side into a time-frequency image. spectrogram (src/GravitationalWaveData/Spectrogram.jl) is a thin wrapper around DSP.spectrogram: a raw, un-averaged STFT power spectrogram (no Welch/median averaging of sub-segments within each column). This tutorial uses a short, heavily-overlapped FFT window to resolve a short-duration transient, then shows why a transient this short can be invisible at the wrong resolution.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Spectrogram"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#Fetching-and-conditioning-real-strain-data","page":"Spectrogram","text":"We reuse the same standard 4096 s H1 segment covering GW150914 that the other tutorials use (cached locally after the first fetch), and the same whitening approach as the Filtering & Whitening tutorial: estimate an ASD from the whole 4096 s segment (long enough that GW150914's own sub-second signal can't meaningfully bias it) and whiten against it, hard-zeroing the response below 20 Hz via whiten's own highpass kwarg. Whitening flattens the spectrum so the STFT below isn't dominated by low-frequency seismic noise – without it, a spectrogram of raw strain shows essentially nothing but that noise floor.\n\ngps_start, gps_end = 1126256640, 1126260736\ngps_merger = 1126259462.4   # GW150914 merger GPS time\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", gps_start, gps_end)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\n\nasd_est = asd(strain; seglen=round(Int, 4 * sample_rate(strain)))\nwhite = whiten(strain, asd_est; highpass=20.0);\n\ncrop down to a 32 s window centered on the merger – \"a few tens of seconds\", per this tutorial's brief, rather than the full 4096 s segment (unnecessary and slow to render as a spectrogram). This window sits deep in the interior of the 4096 s segment we whitened, well clear of the fduration/2-second edge transient whiten leaves at the very start/end of its output (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md's \"Whitening\" section).\n\nseg = crop(white, gps_merger - 16, gps_merger + 16)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(131072 samples, t0=1.1262594463999023e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=32.0s, name=\"strain\")","title":"Fetching and conditioning real strain data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#An-over-dense-STFT-spectrogram","page":"Spectrogram","text":"We use a very short, heavily-overlapped FFT window – 1/16 s segments overlapping by 15/16 – to over-sample the frequency content and give a short transient a chance to stand out. In samples at H1's 4096 Hz sample rate, that's an n=256-sample segment with noverlap=240 (a ~94% overlap between consecutive segments, well above spectrogram's own 50%-overlap default):\n\nn = 256          # 1/16 s at 4096 Hz\nnoverlap = 240   # 15/16 = ~94% overlap\n\nspec = spectrogram(seg; n=n, noverlap=noverlap)\nsize(spec)   # (nfreq bins, ntime bins)\n(129, 8177)\n\nspectrogram returns power; we take its square root to get a more familiar amplitude spectral density scale before plotting, building a new Spectrogram from the square-rooted data (the axis metadata – t0, dt, f0, df – is unchanged by this, only the values):\n\nspec_asd = Spectrogram(sqrt.(spec.data), spec.t0, spec.dt, spec.f0, spec.df;\n                        name=\"Whitened strain ASD\")\nSpectrogram{Float64}(129 freq bins x 8177 time bins, t0=1.1262594464311523e9, dt=0.00390625, f0=0.0, df=16.0, name=\"Whitened strain ASD\")","title":"An over-dense STFT spectrogram"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#A-first,-full-window-look","page":"Spectrogram","text":"The Spectrogram plot recipe defaults to a heatmap with a log-scaled colorbar – appropriate here since STFT power spans many orders of magnitude across frequency. We restrict the frequency axis to 20-1024 Hz (below 20 Hz is already hard-zeroed by whitening's own highpass; above ~1 kHz there's nothing of astrophysical interest for GW150914):\n\nplot(spec_asd; title=\"H1, whitened, over-dense spectrogram (32 s around GW150914)\",\n     ylim=(20, 1024))\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nGW150914's chirp lasts well under a second, out of the 32 s plotted above – so before drawing any conclusion from squinting at the image, it's worth checking numerically whether there's actually anything to see. There is: the single loudest pixel in this entire 32 s, size(spec, 2) = 8177-time-bin spectrogram sits almost exactly at the merger GPS time:\n\nglobal_max, global_idx = findmax(spec_asd.data)\npeak_freq = frequencies(spec_asd)[global_idx[1]]\npeak_time_from_merger = times(spec_asd)[global_idx[2]] - gps_merger\nglobal_max, peak_freq, peak_time_from_merger\n(0.17481874221343502, 64.0, -9.775161743164062e-5)\n\nThat confirms the transient really is there, and roughly where physics says it should be (GW150914's merger frequency is close to that ~64 Hz bin). But at the full 32 s width plotted above, that one bright column is a needle in a haystack of ~8000 columns – easy to miss entirely by eye: at this resolution and dynamic range, a signal this short is practically invisible. The Q-transform tutorial (06_qtransform) is specifically the better tool for finding a sub-second transient like this – it adapts its time/frequency resolution per frequency rather than using one fixed STFT window throughout. Here, we can still get a fair look at it with a plain spectrogram by zooming in.","title":"A first, full-window look"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#Zooming-in-around-the-merger","page":"Spectrogram","text":"GravitationalWaveData.jl doesn't yet provide a dedicated crop for Spectrogram the way it does for TimeSeries/FrequencySeries (docs/CONVENTIONS.md), so we restrict both axes by hand, the same half-open-window idea applied to a matrix instead of a vector:\n\nfunction crop_spectrogram(s::Spectrogram, t_range, f_range)\n    tvals = collect(times(s))\n    fvals = collect(frequencies(s))\n    tmask = t_range[1] .<= tvals .<= t_range[2]\n    fmask = f_range[1] .<= fvals .<= f_range[2]\n    return Spectrogram(s.data[fmask, tmask], first(tvals[tmask]), s.dt, first(fvals[fmask]), s.df;\n                        name=s.name)\nend\n\nzoomed = crop_spectrogram(spec_asd, (gps_merger - 0.15, gps_merger + 0.05), (20.0, 400.0))\nsize(zoomed)\n(24, 51)\n\nRestricting the data itself (not just the plotted axis limits) also tightens the color scale to the range that actually matters here, rather than one set by the 32 s window's overall extremes. The Spectrogram recipe always plots absolute GPS time; for a ~0.2 s window we'd rather read the axis relative to the merger, so we call heatmap directly instead of going through the recipe (the same (freq, time) row/column order Spectrogram stores, and the same colorbar_scale/colorbar_title attributes the recipe sets, just spelled out by hand):\n\nrel_times = collect(times(zoomed)) .- gps_merger\nheatmap(rel_times, collect(frequencies(zoomed)), zoomed.data;\n        xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\",\n        colorbar_scale=:log10, colorbar_title=\"Whitened strain ASD\",\n        title=\"H1 spectrogram around GW150914 (zoomed)\", color=:viridis)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Zooming in around the merger"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/05_spectrogram/#What-this-actually-shows","page":"Spectrogram","text":"Zoomed in like this, a rising streak is visible by eye: power climbs from around 32 Hz roughly 0.1 s before the merger, up through the 60-150 Hz band right around the merger GPS time, then fades within another ~0.03 s – the classic inspiral-merger-ringdown shape of a binary black hole coalescence, and broadly consistent with GW150914's actual reported peak frequency (around 150 Hz). We can quantify the contrast against the background rather than just eyeballing it: comparing the same global peak found above to the whole spectrogram's typical (median) pixel value,\n\nbackground_level = sort(vec(spec_asd.data))[length(spec_asd.data) ÷ 2]\nglobal_max, background_level, global_max / background_level\n(0.17481874221343502, 0.01910758898404444, 9.149178494440887)\n\nthe peak is roughly an order of magnitude above the typical background pixel – a real, if modest, excursion, not an artifact of the color scale. That said, this is a much less dramatic picture than the sharp, high-contrast chirp track a matched-filter-style time-frequency method produces: a fixed-window STFT (even a heavily-overlapped one like this) trades time resolution against frequency resolution the same way at every frequency, whereas GW150914's chirp sweeps through several octaves in a fraction of a second – exactly the regime where a fixed-resolution STFT struggles and a multi-resolution method doesn't. See the Q-transform tutorial (06_qtransform) for that sharper, purpose-built view of the same GW150914 chirp.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"What this actually shows"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#GPS-Time-and-Segments","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"Gravitational-wave pipelines timestamp everything in GPS seconds rather than UTC, and track \"when was the detector taking good data\" with half-open segments, not individual timestamps. This tutorial covers both corners of GravitationalWaveData.jl: GPSTime for converting between the two time systems, and Segments for building and combining data-availability intervals.\n\nGravitationalWaveData.jl doesn't fetch data-quality segments over the network, so the example builds a small, hand-crafted \"instrument locked\" scenario instead: it runs instantly, offline, and shows the segment algebra (union, intersect, gaps) used to find, for example, double-coincident observing time.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Dates","title":"GPS Time & Segments"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#Part-1:-GPS-time-UTC","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"GPS time is a continuous SI-second count from the GPS epoch (1980-01-06T00:00:00 UTC) with no leap seconds inserted after that epoch, unlike UTC. GravitationalWaveData.jl converts between the two via a real historical leap-second table (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md and src/GravitationalWaveData/GPSTime.jl for the full rationale) rather than a single hardcoded offset, since that offset has changed 18 times since 1980 and will change again whenever IERS announces a new leap second.\n\nThe GPS epoch itself is the simplest round-trip: GPS - UTC = 0 there by definition.\n\nGPS_EPOCH\n1980-01-06T00:00:00\n\nutc_to_gps(GPS_EPOCH)\n0.0\n\ngps_to_utc(0.0)\n1980-01-06T00:00:00\n\nleap_seconds reports the current GPS - UTC offset (in whole seconds) as of a given GPS time or UTC DateTime. It is 0 at the epoch and has stepped up by 1 at each of the 18 leap seconds inserted since:\n\nleap_seconds(GPS_EPOCH)\n0\n\nTwo real, published GW event trigger times make good round-trip examples, since they straddle one of those leap-second insertions (2017-01-01, when GPS - UTC stepped from 17s to 18s) and so exercise two different rows of the underlying table:\n\ngw150914_gps = 1_126_259_462.4   # GW150914, before the 2017-01-01 leap second\nleap_seconds(gw150914_gps)\n17\n\ngw150914_utc = gps_to_utc(gw150914_gps)\n2015-09-14T09:50:45.400\n\nutc_to_gps(gw150914_utc) ≈ gw150914_gps\ntrue\n\ngw170817_gps = 1_187_008_882.4   # GW170817, after the 2017-01-01 leap second\nleap_seconds(gw170817_gps)\n18\n\ngps_to_utc(gw170817_gps)\n2017-08-17T12:41:04.400\n\nBoth round-trip exactly (up to DateTime's millisecond resolution limit — GPS time itself can carry sub-millisecond precision, DateTime cannot). The one input gps_to_utc/utc_to_gps can't round-trip meaningfully is an instant that falls exactly on a leap-second insertion itself (the inserted leap second 23:59:60 has no DateTime representation at all, since Julia's Dates module has no notion of leap seconds) — not a concern for real analysis GPS times, which are essentially never chosen to land on that exact boundary.","title":"Part 1: GPS time <-> UTC"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#Part-2:-Segment-and-SegmentList","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"Segment(start, stop) is a half-open [start, stop) GPS-time interval — the same half-open convention TimeSeries/FrequencySeries cropping uses elsewhere in this package, so a boundary sample is never double-counted depending on which API touched it. A SegmentList is a sorted, automatically-coalesced collection of Segments: overlapping or touching segments merge into one on construction, so every SegmentList you hold is already in canonical form.\n\nSuppose H1 and L1 each log a handful of \"interferometer locked\" intervals (GPS-second offsets from an arbitrary reference t0, chosen here so the window brackets GW150914's own trigger time) over a two-hour stretch:\n\nt0 = gw150914_gps - 3600.0   # window starts 1 hour before the GW150914 trigger\ntrigger_offset = gw150914_gps - t0\ntrigger_offset\n3600.0\n\nh1_locked = SegmentList([\n    Segment(0.0, 1200.0),      # locked for the first 20 minutes\n    Segment(1500.0, 3600.0),   # relocks after a 5-minute break...\n    Segment(3600.0, 5400.0),   # ...and holds straight through (touches the previous segment)\n    Segment(6000.0, 7200.0),\n])\nSegmentList(3 segments, total duration=6300.0s)\n\nNote that the middle two segments above were given as separate, touching intervals ([1500,3600) then [3600,5400)) but SegmentList merged them into one on construction, exactly as docs/CONVENTIONS.md describes — touching segments coalesce even though overlaps (below) would say they don't overlap:\n\nh1_locked\nSegmentList(3 segments, total duration=6300.0s)\n\nl1_locked = SegmentList([\n    Segment(600.0, 2400.0),\n    Segment(3000.0, 4800.0),\n    Segment(5000.0, 7200.0),\n])\n\nl1_locked\nSegmentList(3 segments, total duration=5800.0s)\n\noverlaps answers a sharper question than \"do these segments' lists cover common time\" — it tests two individual Segments for a genuinely shared point, which merely touching segments do not have:\n\noverlaps(Segment(0.0, 10.0), Segment(5.0, 15.0))   # true: share (5, 10)\ntrue\n\noverlaps(Segment(0.0, 10.0), Segment(10.0, 20.0))   # false: touch at 10, but half-open excludes it\nfalse","title":"Part 2: Segment and SegmentList"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#Combining-lists:-union,-intersect,-duration","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"union gives every second covered by either instrument — useful for \"when was any data being taken\" — and intersect gives only the double-coincident time both interferometers need for a confident detection:\n\neither_locked = union(h1_locked, l1_locked)\nSegmentList(1 segment, total duration=7200.0s)\n\nboth_locked = intersect(h1_locked, l1_locked)\nSegmentList(5 segments, total duration=4900.0s)\n\nduration totals the covered time of a SegmentList (or the length of a single Segment), so we can quantify the double-coincident duty cycle over this two-hour window directly:\n\nanalysis_window = Segment(0.0, 7200.0)\nduration(both_locked) / duration(analysis_window)\n0.6805555555555556\n\nWas GW150914's own trigger time (trigger_offset above) inside a double-coincident stretch? Segment/SegmentList support in for exactly this query:\n\ntrigger_offset in both_locked\ntrue","title":"Combining lists: union, intersect, duration"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#Gaps","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"gaps returns the complement of a SegmentList — the vetoed/no-data time. Without a bound, only the internal gaps (strictly between the first and last segment) are meaningful:\n\ngaps(h1_locked)\nSegmentList(2 segments, total duration=900.0s)\n\nPassing an explicit bound::Segment computes the complement within that specific window instead, including any leading/trailing gap against the window's own edges — e.g. against the full two-hour analysis_window defined above, H1's [0.0, 7200.0) list happens to run edge-to-edge already, so widen the bound slightly to see leading/trailing gaps appear:\n\ngaps(h1_locked, Segment(-600.0, 7800.0))\nSegmentList(4 segments, total duration=2100.0s)\n\nThe same query on the double-coincident list shows exactly which stretches of the analysis window are not usable for a joint H1+L1 search — the complement of both_locked within analysis_window:\n\ngaps(both_locked, analysis_window)\nSegmentList(5 segments, total duration=2300.0s)","title":"Gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/07_gps_segments/#Tying-the-two-halves-together","page":"GPS Time & Segments","text":"Every offset above is a plain GPS-second count relative to t0; converting a segment boundary back to an absolute, human-readable UTC timestamp is just gps_to_utc(t0 + offset), using exactly the leap-second-aware conversion from Part 1. For instance, the start of the first double-locked stretch:\n\nfirst_both = both_locked[1]\ngps_to_utc(t0 + first_both.start)\n2015-09-14T09:00:45.400\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Tying the two halves together"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Resampling","page":"Resampling","text":"Gravitational-wave pipelines routinely need to change a strain series' sample rate: downsampling to cut data volume and compute cost for analyses that don't need the full bandwidth (most compact-binary-coalescence searches only care about content below a few hundred Hz to a couple kHz), or upsampling to bring several detectors/pipelines onto a common grid. This tutorial demonstrates resample – a genuine port of DSP.jl's polyphase FIR resampler, already configured with GW-appropriate anti-aliasing behavior by default (see src/GravitationalWaveData/Resample.jl's module docstring) – and shows, via a before/after ASD comparison, that resampling doesn't introduce aliasing artifacts below the new Nyquist frequency.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Resampling"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Fetching-data","page":"Resampling","text":"The same standard cross-check segment used throughout this project's docs: 4096 seconds of real H1 strain data surrounding GW150914.\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126260736)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\n\nsample_rate(strain), duration(strain)\n(4096.0, 4096.0)","title":"Fetching data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Downsampling:-4096-Hz-2048-Hz","page":"Resampling","text":"resample wraps DSP.resample's polyphase resampler. DSP.jl designs its own Kaiser-windowed anti-aliasing lowpass filter at the new Nyquist frequency by default (60 dB stopband attenuation) and compensates for that filter's own group delay, so the output stays time-aligned with the input – resample doesn't need to add anything on top of what DSP.resample already provides to be GW-appropriate (see the module docstring in src/GravitationalWaveData/Resample.jl for the full detail, including the exact-integer-ratio vs. arbitrary-ratio code paths). Since both the current and requested rates are exact integers here, it takes DSP's exact-ratio polyphase path rather than the approximate arbitrary-ratio path.\n\nstrain_2k = resample(strain, 2048)\n\nsample_rate(strain_2k), duration(strain_2k)\n(2048.0, 4096.0)\n\nt0 and duration are preserved – only the number of samples and the sample spacing change.","title":"Downsampling: 4096 Hz -> 2048 Hz"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Checking-for-aliasing:-ASD-before-vs.-after","page":"Resampling","text":"If resampling were done naively (e.g. simply dropping every other sample with no anti-aliasing filter first), power above the new Nyquist frequency (1024 Hz, half of 2048 Hz) would fold back (\"alias\") into the passband and corrupt it. Comparing the ASD before and after resampling, over the same frequency band, is a direct way to check this didn't happen: a faithful resampler leaves the spectrum below the new Nyquist frequency essentially unchanged.\n\nBoth ASDs use a 4-second Welch segment length (seglen = 4 * sample_rate), so they land on exactly the same frequency grid (df = 0.25 Hz) and overlay directly with no interpolation needed.\n\nasd_orig = asd(strain; seglen=4 * 4096, overlap=0.5)\nasd_2k = asd(strain_2k; seglen=4 * 2048, overlap=0.5)\n\nband = (10.0, 1024.0)   # up to (not including) the new Nyquist frequency\nasd_orig_band = crop(asd_orig, band...)\nasd_2k_band = crop(asd_2k, band...)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(4056 bins, f0=10.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain ASD\")\n\np = plot(asd_orig_band; label=\"original (4096 Hz)\", color=:black,\n         xlabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\",\n         title=\"ASD before/after downsampling 4096 Hz -> 2048 Hz\")\nplot!(p, asd_2k_band; label=\"resampled (2048 Hz)\", color=:red, linestyle=:dash,\n      ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\")\np\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nThe two curves overlay almost exactly across the whole band, including right up to the new Nyquist edge at 1024 Hz – no aliasing bump or excess power piling up near that edge, which is exactly what a correctly-designed anti-aliasing filter should produce. A closer look at the region right next to the new Nyquist frequency makes this most obvious, since aliasing artifacts (if resample were buggy, or missing its anti-aliasing filter entirely) would show up as excess power right at that edge:\n\nedge_band = (900.0, 1024.0)\np2 = plot(crop(asd_orig, edge_band...); label=\"original (4096 Hz)\", color=:black,\n          xlabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\",\n          title=\"Zoom near the new Nyquist edge (1024 Hz)\")\nplot!(p2, crop(asd_2k, edge_band...); label=\"resampled (2048 Hz)\", color=:red, linestyle=:dash,\n      ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\")\np2\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Checking for aliasing: ASD before vs. after"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Numerical-validation","page":"Resampling","text":"Beyond this visual check, resample has been cross-checked against an independent polyphase resampler (gwpy's .resample()) on this exact GW150914 H1 segment (4096 Hz -> 2048 Hz): median 0.34%, 95th-percentile 0.56% relative PSD difference across the compared band. The only outlier bins (0.3% of them) trace back to a real narrow spectral line around 585-592 Hz in the data itself. See the Validation section of the Conventions page.","title":"Numerical validation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/04_resampling/#Upsampling-works-the-same-way","page":"Resampling","text":"There's nothing downsampling-specific about resample – passing a higher rate than the input's current one upsamples instead, using the same polyphase machinery with the anti-imaging filter now placed at the new (higher) Nyquist frequency. A short crop keeps this demonstration quick:\n\nzoom = crop(strain, 1126256640.0, 1126256650.0)   # first 10 s of the segment\nzoom_8k = resample(zoom, 8192)\n\nsample_rate(zoom), sample_rate(zoom_8k)\n(4096.0, 8192.0)\n\nAs with downsampling, t0 and duration are preserved – only the sample count and spacing change.\n\nduration(zoom), duration(zoom_8k)\n(10.0, 10.0)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Upsampling works the same way"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#GWOSC-and-GWF-Fetch","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"Every other tutorial in this set starts from data that's already local. This one covers how it gets there in the first place: GravitationalWaveData.jl's own network client for the public GWOSC (Gravitational Wave Open Science Center) archive, which fetches real strain data by GPS range or by event name, in whichever file format GWOSC offers. The API is fetch_open_data (two methods — by GPS range, or by event name) plus two file readers: load_gwosc_strain for GWOSC's HDF5 files, and load_gwf_strain/ list_gwf_channels for LIGO/Virgo's native binary frame (\"GWF\") format.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData","title":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#Fetching-by-explicit-GPS-range","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"The most direct way to get a segment: name a detector and a GPS interval. GWOSC's public science-run archive is split into fixed-length (typically 4096 s) files, so this only succeeds when [gps_start, gps_end) falls entirely within one such file — fetch_open_data raises a clear ArgumentError otherwise rather than silently returning a partial/wrong download. This is the same standard H1 segment (GW150914, O1, 4096 s starting at GPS 1126256640) every other tutorial in this set reuses:\n\nh1_path = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126256640 + 4096)\n\"/home/runner/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain/H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.hdf5\"\n\nfetch_open_data only gets bytes onto disk — parsing is a separate step, load_gwosc_strain:\n\nh1_strain, h1_dq = load_gwosc_strain(h1_path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\nduration(h1_strain), sample_rate(h1_strain)\n(4096.0, 4096.0)","title":"Fetching by explicit GPS range"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#Fetching-by-event-name-instead","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"Often you don't know (or don't want to look up) the exact GPS range a science-run file covers — you just know the event's name. The second fetch_open_data method takes (event, detector) and resolves it against GWOSC's event catalog instead of the raw archive:\n\nevent_path = fetch_open_data(\"GW150914\", \"H1\")\n\"/home/runner/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain/H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.hdf5\"\n\nPerhaps surprisingly, this downloads (or, here, re-serves from cache) the exact same 4096 s file as the GPS-range call above:\n\nevent_path == h1_path\ntrue\n\nThe reason is in fetch_open_data's own docstring: an event typically has several catalog releases (versions), and this method tries the highest-version release first, stopping at the first one that has any matching strain file. GW150914's newest release (GWTC-2.1-confident, version=4) only republishes the full 4096 s science segment — it doesn't offer a short quicklook file — so that's what gets returned by default, identical to fetching the GPS range directly.\n\nThe actual short convenience file does exist, just under an older catalog release. Passing duration selects a file of that exact length (seconds), which walks the same version-ordered search but only accepts a match of that duration — landing on GW150914's original 32 s discovery-paper release instead:\n\nshort_path = fetch_open_data(\"GW150914\", \"H1\"; duration=32)\n\"/home/runner/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain/H-H1_GWOSC_4KHZ_R1-1126259447-32.hdf5\"\n\nshort_strain, _ = load_gwosc_strain(short_path)\nduration(short_strain)\n32.0\n\nThis is the easier way to grab a specific well-known event without knowing its exact GPS range or which science-run file contains it — at the cost of needing to know (or discover, as above) which duration the event's catalog actually offers, since not every event publishes a short file under its latest catalog version.","title":"Fetching by event name instead"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#Local-caching","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"Both fetch_open_data methods cache downloaded files under cache_dir (default ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain), keyed by GWOSC's own filename. A repeat call for the same detector/range/sample-rate is served from that cache without re-downloading the (potentially large) strain file — only GWOSC's small locator JSON is re-queried. Timing the GPS-range call from above against a repeat makes this concrete:\n\nt_first = @elapsed fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126256640 + 4096)\nt_repeat = @elapsed fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126256640 + 4096)\n(first_call_seconds=t_first, repeat_call_seconds=t_repeat)\n(first_call_seconds = 0.180508947, repeat_call_seconds = 0.181340326)\n\nBoth calls above land on an already-cached file (this tutorial already fetched it once, and ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain persists across runs), so neither number reflects a genuine ~100+ MB download — but t_repeat is still consistently the cheaper of the two, since even a fully-cached-on-disk t_first still re-resolves the GWOSC archive-links JSON before discovering the local file is already present, while nothing here changes that cost between the two calls other than normal run-to-run variance. The qualitative point holds regardless: rerun this whole tutorial (or any other tutorial reusing this same segment) and the expensive part — the network transfer itself — only ever happens once per cache directory.","title":"Local caching"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#Native-frame-files:-.gwf","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"GWOSC also republishes the same strain data in LIGO/Virgo's own native binary frame format (\"GWF\", the IGWD frame format specified by LIGO-T970130). GravitationalWaveData.jl ships a pure-Julia reader for it (src/GravitationalWaveData/IO/GWF.jl) — no LALSuite/frame-cpp dependency needed. This tutorial uses the same GW150914 H1 segment as above, downloaded (once, then reused) from the exact URL data/README.md documents for it, found via GWOSC's bulk archive-links API:\n\nusing Downloads: Downloads\n\ndata_dir = joinpath(dirname(dirname(pathof(GravitationalWaveData))), \"data\")\nmkpath(data_dir)\ngwf_path = joinpath(data_dir, \"H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.gwf\")\nif !isfile(gwf_path)\n    Downloads.download(\n        \"https://gwosc.org/archive/data/O1/1126170624/H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.gwf\",\n        gwf_path)\nend\ngwf_path\n\"/home/runner/work/GravitationalWaveData.jl/GravitationalWaveData.jl/data/H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.gwf\"\n\nlist_gwf_channels lists every channel a frame file contains, without reading any sample data — GWOSC's public frame files carry the calibrated strain plus two per-second bitmasks:\n\nlist_gwf_channels(gwf_path)\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"H1:LOSC-STRAIN\"\n \"H1:LOSC-DQMASK\"\n \"H1:LOSC-INJMASK\"\n\nload_gwf_strain reads one channel's samples into a TimeSeries, same return type as load_gwosc_strain. With no channel= given, it looks for the unique channel name containing \"STRAIN\":\n\ngwf_strain = load_gwf_strain(gwf_path)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"H1:LOSC-STRAIN\")","title":"Native frame files: .gwf"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#.gwf-and-HDF5-are-interchangeable-inputs","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"Both formats above cover the identical GPS segment, so they should agree exactly — and, per test/gwf.jl's own cross-check, they do: GWOSC's gzip-compressed FrVect samples round-trip losslessly, so this isn't a tolerance-based isapprox, it's genuine bit-for-bit equality against the HDF5 strain fetched earlier in this tutorial:\n\ngwf_strain.t0 == h1_strain.t0, gwf_strain.dt == h1_strain.dt\n(true, true)\n\ngwf_strain.data == h1_strain.data\ntrue\n\nWhichever format a given dataset happens to be published in, both feed the exact same TimeSeries type into the rest of GravitationalWaveData.jl — filtering, PSD estimation, plotting, all of it. The choice of .gwf vs. .hdf5 only matters at this one loading step.","title":".gwf and HDF5 are interchangeable inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/10_gwosc_gwf_fetch/#A-sanity-check-plot","page":"GWOSC & GWF Fetch","text":"A quick visual confirmation that the .gwf-loaded strain is the same real GW150914 data every other tutorial in this set works with (crop to a few seconds around the merger, since 4096 s of raw strain is indistinguishable noise at a glance — see the Getting Started and Filtering & Whitening tutorials for what it takes to pull the actual signal out):\n\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()\n\nmerger_gps = 1126259462.4\nzoom = crop(gwf_strain, merger_gps - 5, merger_gps + 5)\nplot(zoom; title=\"H1 strain around GW150914 (loaded from .gwf)\", ylabel=\"Strain\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"A sanity-check plot"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/02_psd_asd/#PSD-and-ASD-Estimation","page":"PSD & ASD Estimation","text":"One of the principal ways to characterize a gravitational-wave detector's sensitivity is its amplitude spectral density (ASD): how much strain noise the detector has, as a function of frequency. This tutorial estimates the ASD of a real off-source strain segment, then overlays it against the Advanced LIGO design-sensitivity curve to see how far a real, non-ideal detector sits from its design goal.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots","title":"PSD & ASD Estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/02_psd_asd/#Fetching-data","page":"PSD & ASD Estimation","text":"We use the standard segment shared by these tutorials: 4096 seconds of real H1 strain data surrounding GW150914. fetch_open_data downloads (and locally caches) the official GWOSC HDF5 file; load_gwosc_strain reads it into a TimeSeries (plus a data-quality bitmask series we don't need here).\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126260736)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\nstrain\nTimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\")\n\nduration(strain), sample_rate(strain)\n(4096.0, 4096.0)","title":"Fetching data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/02_psd_asd/#Estimating-the-ASD","page":"PSD & ASD Estimation","text":"asd estimates a one-sided amplitude spectral density via median-Welch averaging (Hann-windowed segments, median-of-periodograms across segments with the standard ln(2) debiasing correction – see docs/CONVENTIONS.md's \"PSD/ASD normalization\" section for the exact convention). seglen is given in samples, not seconds – at H1's 4096 Hz sample rate a 4-second segment (with the default 50% overlap) is 4 * 4096 samples.\n\nh1_asd = asd(strain; seglen=4 * 4096, overlap=0.5)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(8193 bins, f0=0.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain ASD\")","title":"Estimating the ASD"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/02_psd_asd/#Reference:-the-aLIGO-design-sensitivity-curve","page":"PSD & ASD Estimation","text":"aligo_design_asd evaluates Advanced LIGO's \"zero-detuning, high-power\" design ASD, ported verbatim from LALSimulation's XLALSimNoisePSDaLIGOZeroDetHighPower (a full quantum-noise\n\nthermal-noise model, not the simpler closed-form \"Ajith fit\" some other codebases use under a\n\nsimilar name – see docs/CONVENTIONS.md). Passing an (f0, df, n) grid rather than a bare frequency vector asks it to return a FrequencySeries aligned exactly to h1_asd's own bins, so the two overlay directly with no interpolation needed.\n\nThe model is only valid above ~9 Hz (it omits seismic/Newtonian-noise terms that dominate below that), and its DC bin is set to exactly 0.0 by convention rather than evaluated (the formula is singular at f=0) – so we restrict the comparison to a physically sensible band before plotting, which conveniently also avoids trying to log-plot a zero.\n\ndesign = aligo_design_asd(h1_asd.f0, h1_asd.df, length(h1_asd))\n\nband = (10.0, 2000.0)\nh1_asd_band = crop(h1_asd, band...)\ndesign_band = crop(design, band...)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(7960 bins, f0=10.0, df=0.25, name=\"aLIGO zero-detuning high-power design ASD\")\n\np = plot(h1_asd_band; label=\"H1 measured ASD\", color=:black,\n         xlabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\",\n         title=\"H1 strain noise vs. aLIGO design sensitivity (GW150914 segment)\")\nplot!(p, design_band; label=\"aLIGO design ASD\", color=:red, linestyle=:dash,\n      ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nThe measured noise floor roughly tracks the shape of the design curve – rising steeply below ~20 Hz (the seismic wall), flattening into a broad sensitive \"bucket\" through the low hundreds of Hz, then rising again at high frequency (shot noise) – but it does not hug the design curve everywhere. This is real early-observing-run (O1-era) data, not a detector already running at its final design sensitivity, so expect the measured curve to sit visibly above the design curve across much of the band, especially away from the most sensitive ~100-300 Hz region. That gap between \"measured\" and \"design\" is itself useful diagnostic information – it's exactly what commissioners track as a detector is tuned toward its design goal over an observing run.","title":"Reference: the aLIGO design-sensitivity curve"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/02_psd_asd/#Virgo's-design-curve","page":"PSD & ASD Estimation","text":"ReferenceCurves.jl also ships an Advanced Virgo design curve, avirgo_design_asd, with the exact same two calling conventions as aligo_design_asd (a plain frequency vector, or an (f0, df, n) grid returning a FrequencySeries). Unlike aLIGO's curve, AdvVirgo's is a simple phenomenological log-polynomial/exponential fit (Manzotti & Dietz 2012) rather than a full quantum-noise derivation – see docs/CONVENTIONS.md for the distinction. Adding it to the same plot is a one-liner:\n\navirgo_band = crop(avirgo_design_asd(h1_asd.f0, h1_asd.df, length(h1_asd)), band...)\nplot!(p, avirgo_band; label=\"AdvVirgo design ASD\", color=:blue, linestyle=:dot,\n      ylabel=\"Strain ASD (1/sqrt(Hz))\")\np\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Virgo's design curve"},{"category":"section","location":"api/data/#Data-types-and-I/O","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"The TimeSeries, FrequencySeries and Spectrogram types, GWOSC and frame-file I/O, the download cache, and published posterior samples.","title":"Data types and I/O"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.FrequencySeries","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"FrequencySeries{T,V<:AbstractVector{T}}\n\nA uniformly-sampled frequency-domain series: data (length N, real or complex T, e.g. the output of FFTW.rfft) sampled every df Hz starting at frequency f0 (usually 0.0). Bin k (1-based) is at frequency f0 + (k-1) * df.\n\nFrequencySeries(data, f0, df; name=nothing)\nFrequencySeries(data, df; name=nothing)   # f0 defaults to 0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.FrequencySeries"},{"category":"type","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.TimeSeries","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"TimeSeries{T,V<:AbstractVector{T}}\n\nA uniformly-sampled time-domain series: data (length N, real or complex T) sampled every dt seconds starting at GPS epoch t0. Sample k (1-based) occurs at GPS time t0 + (k-1) * dt.\n\nV is kept as a free type parameter (rather than hardcoding Vector{T}) so views, ranges, and (eventually) GPU arrays can be stored without an extra copy.\n\nConstruct either with dt or sample_rate:\n\nTimeSeries(data, t0, dt; name=nothing)\nTimeSeries(data, t0; sample_rate=..., name=nothing)\n\nname is optional free-text metadata (e.g. a channel name like \"H1:STRAIN\"); it participates in show but not in arithmetic/equality-of-sampling checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.TimeSeries"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.crop","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"crop(fs::FrequencySeries, f_start=nothing, f_end=nothing) -> FrequencySeries\n\nRestrict fs to the half-open frequency window [f_start, f_end) Hz, snapping each bound to the nearest bin. Either bound may be omitted (nothing, the default) to mean \"first bin\" / \"last bin\" respectively. Throws ArgumentError if the resulting range is empty or falls outside fs's coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.crop"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.crop-2","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"crop(ts::TimeSeries, t_start=nothing, t_end=nothing) -> TimeSeries\n\nRestrict ts to the half-open GPS time window [t_start, t_end), snapping each bound to the nearest sample (the same rounding condition_segment uses). Either bound may be omitted (nothing, the default) to mean \"start of the series\" / \"end of the series\" respectively. Throws ArgumentError if the resulting range is empty or falls outside ts's coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.crop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"duration(ts::TimeSeries) -> Float64\n\nSpan of the series in seconds, length(ts) * ts.dt (i.e. t0 to t0 + duration, half-open – matches length(ts) == round(Int, duration(ts) / ts.dt)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.frequencies-Tuple{FrequencySeries}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"frequencies(fs::FrequencySeries) -> AbstractRange{Float64}\n\nFrequency (Hz) of every bin: f0 .+ df .* (0:length(fs)-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.frequencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.sample_rate-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"sample_rate(ts::TimeSeries) -> Float64\n\n1 / ts.dt, in Hz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.sample_rate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.times-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"times(ts::TimeSeries) -> AbstractRange{Float64}\n\nGPS time of every sample: t0 .+ dt .* (0:length(ts)-1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.times"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_download-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_gwosc_download(url, cache_dir; force=false) -> path\n\nDownload url (a GWOSC strain file or other data-release URL) into cache_dir, named after the URL's own filename (see _url_filename; GWOSC and catalog filenames already encode detector/GPS-start/duration/rate/version, e.g. H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.hdf5, so this is a natural content-addressed cache key). If a same-named file already exists in cache_dir and force=false, the network is skipped entirely and the cached path is returned. Downloads to a .part sibling file first and mvs it into place on success, so a failed/interrupted download never leaves a corrupt file at the cache path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_download"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_event_candidates-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{Nothing, Integer}, AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_gwosc_event_candidates(event, version, host) -> Vector{Pair{String,Any}}\n\nGWOSC catalog entries (key => metadata, metadata including jsonurl) for event, matched against the event commonName, the dataset key, or the GWYYMMDD prefix of the common name, preferring exact commonName matches. Restricted to version if given, and ordered highest-version-first. Throws ArgumentError if nothing matches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_event_candidates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_fetch_json-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_gwosc_fetch_json(url) -> parsed JSON\n\nDownloads.download url into memory and parse it as JSON. Network/HTTP failures (GWOSC unreachable, DNS failure, 404, ...) are converted into a clear ErrorException rather than left as a raw Downloads.RequestError/hang, per this package's convention of failing loudly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._gwosc_fetch_json"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._url_filename-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_url_filename(url) -> String\n\nThe file name a download URL refers to: its last path component, or the one before it for Zenodo-style .../files/<name>/content URLs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC._url_filename"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.condition_segment-Tuple{TimeSeries}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"condition_segment(ts::TimeSeries; trigger_time, duration=4.0, post_trigger=2.0, alpha=0.2)\n    -> TimeSeries\n\nSlice a trigger-relative analysis segment out of a full strain TimeSeries and apply a Tukey window (DSP.Windows.tukey, alpha default matching common LIGO/bilby conditioning practice): duration total seconds, ending post_trigger seconds after trigger_time (so the segment starts at trigger_time - (duration - post_trigger)).\n\nOnly the DATA segment is windowed here – the frequency-domain waveform TEMPLATE evaluated downstream is analytic and is not (and should not be) windowed; this asymmetry (tapered data, un-tapered analytic template) is standard practice in matched-filter GW parameter estimation, not an oversight.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.condition_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.fetch_open_data-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"fetch_open_data(event, detector; sample_rate=4096, duration=nothing, version=nothing,\n                 cache_dir=<user cache>, host=\"https://gwosc.org\", force=false) -> String\n\nDownload the official GWOSC event-data-release strain HDF5 file for event (e.g. \"GW190412\", matched against GWOSC's event commonName/dataset key/GWYYMMDD prefix, following the highest available catalog version unless version is given) and detector, returning a local file path directly consumable by load_gwosc_strain.\n\nEvents typically offer several segment lengths (e.g. a short ~32s release alongside the full science-run file); duration (seconds) selects one exactly, defaulting to the shortest available match at sample_rate (the common case: a small smoke-test/quick-look segment rather than the full 4096s file). See this module's docstring above for the exact GWOSC endpoints queried.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.fetch_open_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.fetch_open_data-Tuple{AbstractString, Real, Real}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"fetch_open_data(detector, gps_start, gps_end; sample_rate=4096, version=nothing,\n                 cache_dir=<user cache>, host=\"https://gwosc.org\", force=false) -> String\n\nDownload the official GWOSC science-run strain HDF5 file whose coverage fully contains [gps_start, gps_end) for detector (e.g. \"H1\", \"L1\", \"V1\"), returning a local file path directly consumable by load_gwosc_strain – this function only gets the bytes onto disk, it doesn't parse them (that's already solved).\n\nGWOSC's public science-run archive is split into fixed-length (typically 4096s) segment files, so this only succeeds when the requested range falls within a single such segment; it raises a clear ArgumentError (not a partial/silently-wrong download) if no single hdf5 file at sample_rate fully covers the request. version, if given, additionally requires the chosen file's name to carry that data-release version tag (_V<version>-/_R<version>-, GWOSC's own file-naming convention).\n\nDownloaded files are cached under cache_dir (default gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir(\"strain\"), i.e. ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain unless GRAVITATIONALWAVEDATA_CACHE_DIR or XDG_CACHE_HOME is set; see gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir) keyed by GWOSC's own filename; a repeat call for the same file re-does the (cheap, small-JSON) locator query but skips re-downloading the (potentially large) strain file itself, unless force=true. See this module's docstring above for the exact GWOSC endpoints queried.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.fetch_open_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir-Tuple{Vararg{AbstractString}}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir(subdirs...) -> String\n\nRoot of GravitationalWaveData.jl's download cache, optionally joined with subdirs. Resolved in order:\n\nENV[\"GRAVITATIONALWAVEDATA_CACHE_DIR\"], if set;\n$XDG_CACHE_HOME/gravitationalwavedata, if XDG_CACHE_HOME is set;\n~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata.\n\nThe directory is a plain tree of files under their upstream names, so other tools can read it directly. Subdirectories: strain/ holds GWOSC strain files downloaded by fetch_open_data, and posteriors/ holds parameter-estimation releases downloaded by fetch_posterior_samples. The path is not created by this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.load_gwosc_strain-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"load_gwosc_strain(path) -> (strain::TimeSeries{Float64}, dq_mask::TimeSeries)\n\nRead a standard GWOSC/LOSC HDF5 strain file, returning two TimeSeries:\n\nstrain: the /strain/Strain dataset, with t0/dt taken from that dataset's own Xstart/Xspacing attributes (not /meta/GPSstart directly – both agree in every file checked so far, but the dataset's own attributes are the authoritative source for the sample grid).\ndq_mask: the per-second data-quality bitmask /quality/simple/DQmask, as its own TimeSeries anchored to the same GPS epoch as strain but with its own (typically 1.0s) sample spacing read from the file rather than assumed.\n\nThe DQ mask is returned as a second TimeSeries rather than bundled as metadata on strain, since it is itself a uniformly-sampled GPS-anchored series (just at a coarser rate) and gets times()/crop() for free this way – e.g. crop(dq_mask, t0, t1) to pull the DQ bits covering an analysis segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWOSC.load_gwosc_strain"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.fetch_posterior_samples-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"fetch_posterior_samples(event; version=nothing, cache_dir=gravitationalwavedata_cache_dir(\"posteriors\"),\n                        host=\"https://gwosc.org\", force=false) -> String\n\nDownload the preferred published posterior-sample release for event (e.g. \"GW150914\", matched as in fetch_open_data's event method) and return the local file path. GWOSC catalog versions are tried highest-first, and the first one whose preferred PE analysis has an HDF5 data release is used, so recent events resolve to their GWTC-2.1/GWTC-3 PESummary files. version restricts the search to one GWOSC event version.\n\nFiles are cached under cache_dir by their upstream file name; a cached file is returned without re-downloading unless force=true. Read the result with list_posterior_analyses and load_posterior_samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.fetch_posterior_samples"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.list_posterior_analyses-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"list_posterior_analyses(path) -> Vector{String}\n\nNames of the analyses in a PESummary posterior file (the top-level groups that contain a posterior_samples dataset), e.g. [\"C01:IMRPhenomXPHM\", \"C01:Mixed\", \"C01:SEOBNRv4PHM\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.list_posterior_analyses"},{"category":"function","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.load_posterior_samples","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"load_posterior_samples(path, analysis=nothing) -> Dict{String,Vector{Float64}}\n\nRead one analysis's posterior samples from a PESummary file as a column dictionary, parameter name => samples (e.g. \"chirp_mass\", \"mass_ratio\", \"luminosity_distance\", \"ra\", \"dec\", ...; names follow the bilby/PESummary conventions of the release). analysis defaults to \"C01:Mixed\" when present (the combined-waveform result), otherwise the only analysis in the file; see list_posterior_analyses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Posteriors.load_posterior_samples"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF._decompress_frvect-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{UInt8}, Integer, Type{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_decompress_frvect(raw::Vector{UInt8}, compress::Integer, ::Type{T}, nData::Integer) -> Vector{T}\n\nTurn an FrVect's on-disk data bytes into nData samples of type T. Only compress codes 0/256 (raw) and 1/257 (gzip) are implemented – confirmed (see module docstring) to be the only schemes GWOSC's public data actually uses; every other code in _COMPRESS_NAMES raises a clear error instead of silently misinterpreting zero-suppressed or gzip-differential data as raw bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF._decompress_frvect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_fradcdata_header-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_parse_fradcdata_header(buf, p) -> (name::String, time_offset::Float64, next_p)\n\nReads just enough of an FrAdcData (Table 10: name STRING, comment STRING, channelGroup INT_4U, channelNumber INT_4U, nBits INT_4U, bias REAL_4, slope REAL_4, units STRING, sampleRate REAL_8, timeOffset REAL_8, ...) to identify the channel and its time offset – same rationale as _parse_frprocdata_header above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_fradcdata_header"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_frameh_gtime-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_parse_frameh_gtime(buf, p) -> (GTimeS::UInt32, GTimeN::UInt32, next_p)\n\nReads just the fields of FrameH (Table 9) needed for absolute timing: name STRING, run INT_4S, frame INT_4U, dataQuality INT_4U, GTimeS INT_4U, GTimeN INT_4U. Doesn't parse ULeapS/dt/the 13 trailing PTR_STRUCT pointers – unneeded here, since (per the module docstring) this reader matches channels to their FrVect by name across the whole file rather than following FrameH's pointer graph.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_frameh_gtime"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_frprocdata_header-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_parse_frprocdata_header(buf, p) -> (name::String, time_offset::Float64, next_p)\n\nReads just enough of an FrProcData (Table 17: name STRING, comment STRING, type INT_2U, subType INT_2U, timeOffset REAL_8, ...) to identify the channel and its time offset within the frame – the rest of the structure (tRange, fShift, ..., the FrVect/FrTable PTR_STRUCT pointers) isn't needed since this reader locates a channel's FrVect by name (see module docstring), not by following pointers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF._parse_frprocdata_header"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF._read_string-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"_read_string(buf, p) -> (String, next_p)\n\nIGWD STRING type: an INT_2U byte count n (the following char data's length, INCLUDING its mandatory trailing '\\0'), followed by n bytes of ASCII ending in that '\\0'. Returns the string with the terminator stripped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF._read_string"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF.list_gwf_channels-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"list_gwf_channels(path) -> Vector{String}\n\nList the channel names (FrProcData/FrAdcData) found in a GWF frame file, in file order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF.list_gwf_channels"},{"category":"method","location":"api/data/#GravitationalWaveData.GWF.load_gwf_strain-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Data types & I/O","text":"load_gwf_strain(path; channel=nothing) -> TimeSeries{Float64}\n\nRead a real-valued time-series channel out of a LIGO/Virgo IGWD frame (.gwf) file, following LIGO-T970130-v2 (see this module's docstring for the exact subset of the spec implemented and its scope limits: little-endian frames, FrProcData/FrAdcData channels matched to their FrVect by name rather than by resolving the full pointer graph, raw/gzip compression only).\n\nchannel, if given, must exactly match one of list_gwf_channels(path). If omitted, this looks for a unique channel whose name contains \"STRAIN\" (case-insensitive) – GWOSC's convention for its public strain channels (e.g. \"H1:LOSC-STRAIN\", \"H1:GWOSC-4KHZ_R1_STRAIN\") – and errors, listing the channels found, if that isn't unique.\n\nA multi-frame file's chunks for the chosen channel are concatenated in file order; this errors if their sample spacing (dt) differs, or if consecutive chunks aren't exactly time-contiguous (no gap-filling/overlap-resolution is attempted).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GWF.load_gwf_strain"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/08_detector_response/#Detector-Response","page":"Detector Response","text":"A gravitational-wave interferometer is not a uniformly sensitive microphone for spacetime strain: how strongly a given wave registers depends on where on the sky it comes from, how the wave is polarized, and (because the Earth rotates under a fixed detector) what time it arrives. This is the antenna pattern. It matters for two very practical reasons: a single detector can be sitting near a null in its own pattern for a particular source and see almost nothing of an otherwise loud signal, and comparing when a wave arrives at several detectors (each seeing the wavefront at a slightly different geocentric time delay, set by their physical separation) is the primary way multi-detector networks localize a source on the sky.\n\nThe detector modules (src/GravitationalWaveData/Detectors.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/SiderealTime.jl) are a direct, verbatim port of LALSuite's own hardcoded detector table and XLALComputeDetAMResponse/ XLALTimeDelayFromEarthCenter, so results here are bit-for-bit comparable (up to the Float64-vs-REAL4 precision difference noted in Detectors.jl's header) with LALSuite and the pipelines built on it.\n\nEverything below is pure geometry and runs instantly, offline — no GWOSC network fetch needed.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots","title":"Detector Response"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/08_detector_response/#Detector-geometry","page":"Detector Response","text":"GravitationalWaveData.jl ships three ready-made Detector instances — H1 (LIGO Hanford), L1 (LIGO Livingston), and V1 (Virgo) — each a channel-name prefix plus a DetectorGeometry (returned by geometry) holding an Earth-centered Cartesian vertex location (meters) and a dimensionless response tensor built from the detector's two arm directions. The geometry is plain bits, so GPU kernels take geometry(H1) rather than H1. Both are ported verbatim from LALSuite's lalCachedDetectors table (see src/GravitationalWaveData/Detectors.jl's module docstring for the exact source), not re-derived from published latitude/longitude/azimuth numbers, so this module can't drift from LALSuite's own values.\n\nH1\nDetector(\"H1\")\n\ngeometry(H1).location   # meters, Earth-centered Cartesian (ECEF-like)\n(-2.16141492636e6, -3.83469517889e6, 4.60035022664e6)\n\nThe vertex location alone is enough to recover H1's approximate geocentric latitude/longitude (this is the geocentric angle from the Cartesian vector, a close but not identical quantity to the geodetic latitude usually quoted for LIGO Hanford, ~46.45 N, since Earth is an oblate spheroid, not a sphere):\n\nh1_r = sqrt(sum(geometry(H1).location .^ 2))\nh1_lat_deg = rad2deg(asin(geometry(H1).location[3] / h1_r))\nh1_lon_deg = rad2deg(atan(geometry(H1).location[2], geometry(H1).location[1]))\n(h1_lat_deg, h1_lon_deg)\n(46.262943153292014, -119.40765713887839)\n\nThe response tensor is what actually contracts against a wave's polarization to produce the antenna pattern below — it encodes both arms' orientation in one object, following Anderson et al., PRD 63, 042003 (2001):\n\ngeometry(H1).response\n((-0.39261410005855846, -0.07761341236296912, -0.24738904467357226), (-0.07761341236296912, 0.31952406685263823, 0.2279978330803575), (-0.24738904467357226, 0.2279978330803575, 0.07309003320433549))","title":"Detector geometry"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/08_detector_response/#Antenna-response:-F/Fx-at-a-few-sky-positions","page":"Detector Response","text":"compute_antenna_response(detector, ra, dec, psi, gps_time) -> (Fplus, Fcross) gives the plus- and cross-polarization antenna pattern factors for a wave from equatorial sky position (ra, dec) (radians), polarization angle psi (radians), arriving at gps_time (GPS seconds). We use GW150914's own trigger time throughout so every number below is anchored to a real, familiar event, even though nothing here needs real strain data.\n\ngps = 1_126_259_462.4   # GW150914 trigger time (GPS seconds)\n1.1262594624e9\n\nThe point directly overhead H1 (\"zenith\") at this instant has right ascension equal to the local Greenwich hour angle condition gha = 0, i.e. ra = gmst(gps_time), and declination equal to H1's own (geocentric) latitude computed above:\n\nra_zenith = mod2pi(gmst(gps))\ndec_zenith = deg2rad(h1_lat_deg)\n(rad2deg(ra_zenith), rad2deg(dec_zenith))\n(140.74897620710416, 46.262943153292014)\n\nFplus_zenith, Fcross_zenith = compute_antenna_response(H1, ra_zenith, dec_zenith, 0.0, gps)\n(Fplus_zenith, Fcross_zenith)\n(0.24239938194006283, -0.4274070477300541)\n\nEven directly overhead, F+/Fx are nowhere near their maximum possible magnitude of 1 — a quadrupolar antenna pattern is never uniformly \"loudest\" straight up, unlike e.g. a dish antenna. Now a sky position near H1's horizon at the same instant (right ascension offset 90 degrees from the zenith point, declination near the celestial equator):\n\nra_horizon = mod2pi(gmst(gps) + pi / 2)\ndec_horizon = 0.0\nFplus_horizon, Fcross_horizon = compute_antenna_response(H1, ra_horizon, dec_horizon, 0.0, gps)\n(Fplus_horizon, Fcross_horizon)\n(-0.46570413326293203, -0.49477808934703277)\n\nFor this particular pair of points the near-horizon response is actually larger in magnitude than the overhead one — a reminder that \"near the horizon\" doesn't uniformly mean \"less sensitive\" the way it would for, say, a radio dish; the quadrupolar pattern's peaks and nulls are scattered across the whole sky, which is exactly what the full sky map below makes visible.\n\nA convenient single number to compare sky positions by is the quadrature sum sqrt(Fplus^2 + Fcross^2), an overall (polarization-averaged-ish) sensitivity scale bounded in [0, 1]:\n\nsqrt(Fplus_zenith^2 + Fcross_zenith^2), sqrt(Fplus_horizon^2 + Fcross_horizon^2)\n(0.49135958809638103, 0.6794745745324685)","title":"Antenna response: F+/Fx at a few sky positions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/08_detector_response/#The-full-antenna-pattern-across-the-sky","page":"Detector Response","text":"Sweeping (ra, dec) over the whole sky at fixed gps_time/psi and plotting sqrt(Fplus^2 + Fcross^2) as a heatmap reveals H1's characteristic four-lobed quadrupolar pattern directly, rather than spot-checking a handful of points.\n\nras = range(0, 2pi; length=181)\ndecs = range(-pi / 2, pi / 2; length=91)\n\nsensitivity = [\n    let (fp, fc) = compute_antenna_response(H1, ra, dec, 0.0, gps)\n        sqrt(fp^2 + fc^2)\n    end\n    for dec in decs, ra in ras\n]\nsize(sensitivity)   # (length(decs), length(ras)) -- rows=declination, columns=right ascension\n(91, 181)\n\np1 = heatmap(rad2deg.(ras), rad2deg.(decs), sensitivity;\n             xlabel=\"Right ascension (deg)\", ylabel=\"Declination (deg)\",\n             title=\"H1 antenna sensitivity sqrt(F+^2 + Fx^2), psi=0, GPS=$(gps)\",\n             color=:viridis, colorbar_title=\"sensitivity\");\n\nThe bright lobes are sky directions H1 is most sensitive to for this polarization angle; the dark bands running between them are near-nulls (sqrt(Fplus^2 + Fcross^2) close to 0) where a wave from that direction would barely register at all, regardless of its intrinsic amplitude. Changing psi rotates this whole pattern in place (the polarization angle is a genuine third degree of freedom the pattern depends on, not just decoration) — a wave from a fixed sky position can shift from near-null to near-peak response purely as psi varies:\n\np1\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nA 1-D slice at fixed declination makes the same lobed structure easy to read off as a plain line plot instead of a heatmap — here, the sensitivity profile along the celestial equator (dec = 0) as right ascension sweeps through a full rotation:\n\nequator_sensitivity = [\n    let (fp, fc) = compute_antenna_response(H1, ra, 0.0, 0.0, gps)\n        sqrt(fp^2 + fc^2)\n    end\n    for ra in ras\n]\n\nplot(rad2deg.(ras), equator_sensitivity;\n     xlabel=\"Right ascension (deg)\", ylabel=\"sqrt(F+^2 + Fx^2)\",\n     title=\"H1 antenna sensitivity along the celestial equator (dec=0)\",\n     label=\"H1\", legend=:bottomright)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"The full antenna pattern across the sky"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/08_detector_response/#Time-delay-from-geocenter","page":"Detector Response","text":"time_delay_from_geocenter(detector, ra, dec, gps_time) gives the light-travel-time offset (seconds) between the geocenter and a detector for a wave from (ra, dec) arriving at gps_time — the geometric quantity underlying multi-detector coincidence/localization: a real signal shows up in each detector's data offset by (approximately, ignoring the wave's own finite-frequency phase evolution) exactly this amount relative to when it crosses the Earth's center.\n\nSign convention (matches LALSuite exactly, per docs/CONVENTIONS.md and this function's own docstring): positive means the wavefront arrives at the detector after the geocenter — i.e. t_detector = t_geocenter + time_delay_from_geocenter(...).\n\nUsing the same zenith-at-H1 sky position from above, evaluated for all three detectors at the shared GW150914 trigger time:\n\nfor (name, det) in ((\"H1\", H1), (\"L1\", L1), (\"V1\", V1))\n    td = time_delay_from_geocenter(det, ra_zenith, dec_zenith, gps)\n    println(name, \": \", round(td * 1000; digits=3), \" ms\")\nend\nH1: -6.103 ms\nL1: -7.599 ms\nV1: -21.037 ms\n\nH1's own delay for its own zenith direction is small (a wave from directly overhead arrives at H1 close to when it arrives at the geocenter, since H1 sits only one Earth-radius away from center along that exact line — the delay isn't exactly zero because the light-travel geometry depends on the full 3-D vertex location, not just distance-from-center along the local vertical). L1 and V1, sitting thousands of kilometers away from H1 and from each other, see a wave from that same direction at meaningfully different times — this is precisely the information a multi-detector network uses for sky localization: for a given pair of detectors, every sky direction consistent with an observed arrival-time difference traces out a ring on the sky (a cone of constant delay), and intersecting the rings from several detector pairs narrows a source down to a small patch, in the same spirit as GPS trilateration.\n\nSweeping right ascension at fixed declination (again dec = 0, the celestial equator) shows how each detector's delay traces out its own distinct sinusoid-like curve as the source direction rotates through the sky — the pairwise differences between these three curves at any given right ascension is exactly the coincidence-timing information a localization pipeline uses:\n\ndelays = Dict(\n    name => [time_delay_from_geocenter(det, ra, 0.0, gps) * 1000 for ra in ras]\n    for (name, det) in ((\"H1\", H1), (\"L1\", L1), (\"V1\", V1))\n)\n\np2 = plot(rad2deg.(ras), delays[\"H1\"]; label=\"H1\", xlabel=\"Right ascension (deg)\",\n          ylabel=\"Time delay from geocenter (ms)\",\n          title=\"Geocenter time delay along the celestial equator (dec=0), GPS=$(gps)\")\nplot!(p2, rad2deg.(ras), delays[\"L1\"]; label=\"L1\", ylabel=\"Time delay from geocenter (ms)\")\nplot!(p2, rad2deg.(ras), delays[\"V1\"]; label=\"V1\", ylabel=\"Time delay from geocenter (ms)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Time delay from geocenter"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/06_qtransform/#Q-transform","page":"Q-transform","text":"One of the most useful tools for visualizing short-duration features in a TimeSeries – a transient noise glitch, or a gravitational-wave signal – is the constant-Q transform. This is the \"Q-scan\", a staple of LIGO/Virgo detector characterization, and the algorithm behind the single most recognizable image in gravitational-wave astronomy: the upward-sweeping \"chirp\" track of GW150914, bottom panel of Figure 1 in Abbott et al. (2016), the discovery paper.\n\nq_scan/q_transform/qscan_peak (src/GravitationalWaveData/QTransform.jl) implement the Omega-pipeline constant-Q transform (Chatterji 2005) with the same tiling, window and indexing conventions as other Omega-derived implementations. Both the individual tile computations and the full GW150914 peak read-out are numerically cross-checked (see the Conventions page).\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Q-transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/06_qtransform/#Fetching-real-strain-data","page":"Q-transform","text":"The same standard 4096 s off-source segment covering GW150914 that the rest of this project's tutorials use, fetched live from GWOSC via fetch_open_data (cached locally under ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain, so re-running this tutorial doesn't re-download the file):\n\ngps_start, gps_end = 1126256640, 1126260736\ngps_merger = 1126259462.4   # GW150914 merger GPS time\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", gps_start, gps_end)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\nA Q-transform's cost grows with the input duration (more tiles per frequency row), and a full 4096-second scan is neither necessary nor how this is normally done – the standard approach is to crop to a short window around the time of interest first. 16 s centered on the merger is generous enough to resolve frequencies down into the detector's sensitive band while keeping the transform itself fast:\n\nseg = crop(strain, gps_merger - 8.0, gps_merger + 8.0)\n(duration(seg), sample_rate(seg))\n(16.0, 4096.0)","title":"Fetching real strain data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/06_qtransform/#Running-the-multi-Q-search","page":"Q-transform","text":"q_transform FFTs the segment once (normalized_fft), then evaluates a whole family of \"Q-planes\" – one energy grid per Q value, densely log-spaced across qrange – and keeps only the plane containing the single loudest tile. We restrict frange to 20-500 Hz, which both speeds up the search (far fewer tile rows to compute) and matches aLIGO's most sensitive band; search restricts peak-finding (not the computed grid) to a window straddling the merger, so an unrelated loud tile elsewhere in the 16 s segment can't be mistaken for the signal.\n\nscan, peak = q_transform(seg; frange=(20.0, 500.0),\n                          search=(gps_merger - 0.2, gps_merger + 0.1))\npeak\nQPeak(1.1262594624213867e9, 140.3125, 88.87990623881669, 13.332659617556933, 5.65685424949238)\n\nqscan_peak returns the loudest tile as a QPeak: its GPS time, frequency, normalized energy, snr = sqrt(2*energy), and the q of the plane it came from. The search picked out a tile within milliseconds of the known merger time, at a Q close to the ~5.3 value independently reported for GW150914 in the literature – a good sanity check that the multi-Q search is doing something physically sensible, not just numerically self-consistent:\n\nprintln(\"peak time - merger = $(round(peak.time - gps_merger; digits=4)) s\")\nprintln(\"peak frequency     = $(round(peak.frequency; digits=1)) Hz\")\nprintln(\"peak Q              = $(round(peak.q; digits=2))\")\nprintln(\"peak SNR            = $(round(peak.snr; digits=2))\")\npeak time - merger = 0.0214 s\npeak frequency     = 140.3 Hz\npeak Q              = 5.66\npeak SNR            = 13.33","title":"Running the multi-Q search"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/06_qtransform/#Plotting-the-time-frequency-energy-map","page":"Q-transform","text":"scan.rows holds the winning Q-plane's raw energy grid: one row per tile frequency, each with its own time resolution (rows at higher frequencies get finer time sampling – see QScan's docstring). Re-gridding onto a single regular time/frequency axis is a presentation step that doesn't change which tile is loudest or its energy/time/frequency, so the package leaves it to the caller. For this plot we only need something visual, so a short linear-interpolation helper (not part of the package – just enough for a heatmap) resamples every row onto one shared time grid around the merger:\n\nfunction _lininterp(xs, ys, x)\n    x <= xs[1] && return ys[1]\n    x >= xs[end] && return ys[end]\n    i = searchsortedlast(xs, x)\n    x0, x1 = xs[i], xs[i + 1]\n    y0, y1 = ys[i], ys[i + 1]\n    return y0 + (y1 - y0) * (x - x0) / (x1 - x0)\nend\n\nfunction qscan_grid(scan, tlo, thi; ntime=400)\n    common_times = range(tlo, thi; length=ntime)\n    freqs = [row.frequency for row in scan.rows]\n    z = Array{Float64}(undef, length(scan.rows), ntime)\n    for (i, row) in enumerate(scan.rows)\n        n = length(row.energy)\n        row_times = row.t0 .+ row.dt .* (0:(n - 1))\n        z[i, :] .= [_lininterp(row_times, row.energy, t) for t in common_times]\n    end\n    return collect(common_times), freqs, z\nend\n\ngrid_times, grid_freqs, energy_grid = qscan_grid(scan, gps_merger - 0.15, gps_merger + 0.08)\nrel_times = grid_times .- gps_merger;\n\nNow the classic chirp plot: a heatmap of normalized energy against time (relative to merger) and log-scaled frequency:\n\nheatmap(rel_times, grid_freqs, energy_grid;\n        xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", yscale=:log10,\n        colorbar_title=\"Normalized energy\", color=:viridis,\n        title=\"H1 Q-transform, GW150914 (Q=$(round(peak.q; digits=2)))\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nThe bright track sweeping upward through frequency, right at the merger time, is the gravitational-wave chirp – the same feature Figure 1 of Abbott et al. (2016) shows, produced here entirely with GravitationalWaveData.jl. The 12_gw150914_capstone tutorial builds on this together with bandpass/whiten (see 03_filtering_whitening) to reproduce that discovery-paper figure more fully.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Plotting the time-frequency energy map"},{"category":"section","location":"api/processing/#Filtering-and-resampling","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"Butterworth and notch filters, whitening, and polyphase resampling.","title":"Filtering and resampling"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._convolve_same-Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Vector{Float64}}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_convolve_same(a, b) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nFull linear convolution of a and b, cropped to scipy.signal.fftconvolve(a, b, mode=\"same\")'s convention: the length(a)-sample slice of the full (length(a)+length(b)-1) convolution, centered (start = (full_len - length(a)) ÷ 2, floor division, 0-based in scipy's own formula).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._convolve_same"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._fir_from_transfer-Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Integer}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_fir_from_transfer(transfer, ntaps; window=:hann, ncorner=0) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDirect port of gwpy's filter_design.fir_from_transfer: design a length-ntaps (should be even) FIR filter realizing the given one-sided (rfft-style, DC-first) frequency-domain transfer function (here always 1 ./ asd, see whiten), via truncate -> inverse-FFT -> truncate -> circularly-shift-to-center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._fir_from_transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._interpolate-Tuple{FrequencySeries, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_interpolate(fs::FrequencySeries, new_df::Real) -> FrequencySeries\n\nDirect port of gwpy's FrequencySeries.interpolate: linear interpolation (numpy.interp) onto a new frequency grid with spacing new_df, same f0, length round((N-1)*(df/new_df)) + 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._interpolate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._numpy_interp-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:Real}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_numpy_interp(xnew, x, y) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n1-D linear interpolation matching numpy.interp's convention: x must be sorted ascending; xnew values outside [x[1], x[end]] are clamped to the corresponding boundary y value (no extrapolation), matching what FrequencySeries.interpolate relies on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._numpy_interp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._overlap_save_convolve-Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Vector{Float64}, Symbol}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_overlap_save_convolve(data, fir, window) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDirect port of gwpy's TimeSeries.convolve (overlap-save FFT convolution with edge tapering to suppress spectral leakage from the filter's settle-in transient at the two boundaries). For the segment lengths this package's whitening is normally used on (a handful of seconds to a few hundred seconds, fir a couple of seconds long), the nfft >= n/2 branch (single fftconvolve call) is what actually executes; the chunked overlap-save branch is exercised by test/runtests.jl's dedicated long-signal test (forcing n well past 8*length(fir)) checked against the single-shot path, since real whitening usage on this package's typical segment lengths never reaches it otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._overlap_save_convolve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._periodic_hann-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_periodic_hann(n) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe periodic/DFT-even Hann window scipy.signal.get_window(\"hann\", n) returns by default (fftbins=true) – distinct from DSP.Windows.hanning(n), which defaults to the symmetric convention (fftbins=false). Computed directly as 0.5*(1-cos(2*pi*k/n)) for k in 0:n-1, which is exactly a length-(n+1) symmetric Hann window with its last (duplicate-of-first) sample dropped – the standard periodic-window trick. gwpy relies on this specific convention (via scipy.signal.get_window's default) in _truncate_impulse and TimeSeries.convolve's edge tapers, so it's reproduced exactly here rather than approximated with DSP.Windows.hanning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._periodic_hann"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._truncate_impulse-Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Integer, Symbol}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_truncate_impulse(impulse, ntaps, window) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDirect port of gwpy's filter_design._truncate_impulse: window and zero the impulse response so only ntaps total non-tapered-to-zero samples remain (split between the two ends, wrapped around what will become the FIR's center once rolled in _fir_from_transfer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._truncate_impulse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._truncate_transfer-Tuple{Vector{Float64}, Integer}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"_truncate_transfer(transfer, ncorner) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDirect port of gwpy's filter_design._truncate_transfer: zero the first ncorner bins (hard highpass) and smoothly taper 5 samples at each edge of the remaining array with a symmetric (sym=true-equivalent) Tukey(alpha=1) == Hann(10) window, so the transfer function doesn't have a sharp discontinuity at its own array boundaries before being inverse-FFT'd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering._truncate_transfer"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.bandpass-Tuple{TimeSeries, Real, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"bandpass(ts::TimeSeries, flow::Real, fhigh::Real; order::Integer=4) -> TimeSeries\n\nZero-phase Butterworth band-pass filter, passing frequencies in (flow, fhigh) Hz. Filter design via DSP.digitalfilter(DSP.Bandpass(flow, fhigh), DSP.Butterworth(order); fs=sample_rate(ts)), applied with DSP.filtfilt (forward-backward, so the effective order is 2*order and there is no phase distortion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.bandpass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.highpass-Tuple{TimeSeries, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"highpass(ts::TimeSeries, fcut::Real; order::Integer=4) -> TimeSeries\n\nZero-phase Butterworth high-pass filter with corner frequency fcut Hz. See bandpass for the design/application convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.highpass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.lowpass-Tuple{TimeSeries, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"lowpass(ts::TimeSeries, fcut::Real; order::Integer=4) -> TimeSeries\n\nZero-phase Butterworth low-pass filter with corner frequency fcut Hz. See bandpass for the design/application convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.lowpass"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.notch-Tuple{TimeSeries, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"notch(ts::TimeSeries, f0::Real; bandwidth::Real=1.0, order::Integer=4) -> TimeSeries\n\nZero-phase Butterworth band-stop (notch) filter, rejecting a narrow band (f0 - bandwidth/2, f0 + bandwidth/2) Hz – e.g. mains-hum removal at f0=60 (US) or f0=50 (EU). Same Butterworth+filtfilt convention as bandpass, using DSP.Bandstop + DSP.Butterworth.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.notch"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.whiten-Tuple{TimeSeries, FrequencySeries}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"whiten(ts::TimeSeries, psd_or_asd::FrequencySeries; kind=:asd, fduration=2.0,\n       highpass=nothing, window=:hann) -> TimeSeries\n\nWhiten ts using inverse spectrum truncation: design a length-fduration (seconds) time-domain FIR filter approximating 1/ASD(f) (via _fir_from_transfer) and convolve it with the detrended data using the overlap-save method (via _overlap_save_convolve) – not a naive FFT/divide/inverse-FFT, which would ring badly at the segment edges.\n\npsd_or_asd supplies the spectrum to whiten against, typically from asd or psd on an off-source stretch; pass kind=:psd if you're passing a power (not amplitude) spectral density. It is linearly interpolated onto the frequency grid with spacing 1/duration(ts) before use, so it need not already be sampled at that resolution (e.g. it's fine to reuse the same off-source-estimated ASD for whitening several different on-source segments).\n\nhighpass (Hz), if given, hard-zeros the FIR's frequency response below that corner in addition to whatever 1/ASD already does there.\n\nOn stationary Gaussian input, the output has zero mean and unit variance. A segment of length fduration/2 at each end of the output is corrupted by filter settle-in (tapered instead of exact) – crop it off if that matters for downstream use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Filtering.whiten"},{"category":"method","location":"api/processing/#GravitationalWaveData.Resample.resample-Tuple{TimeSeries, Real}","page":"Filtering & Resampling","text":"resample(ts::TimeSeries, new_rate::Real; attenuation::Real=60, rel_bw::Real=1.0,\n          Nphi::Integer=32) -> TimeSeries\n\nResample ts to new_rate Hz using DSP.resample's polyphase FIR resampler, with an automatically-designed Kaiser-window anti-aliasing (when downsampling) / anti-imaging (when upsampling) lowpass filter at the new Nyquist frequency – matching DSP.resample's own defaults, which are already GW-appropriate (see module docs above).\n\nattenuation: stopband attenuation of the anti-aliasing filter, in dB (DSP.resample_filter's default is 60).\nrel_bw: filter cutoff as a fraction of the new Nyquist frequency (DSP.resample_filter's default is 1.0, i.e. cutoff exactly at the new Nyquist).\nNphi: number of polyphase filter phases, only used for the arbitrary-ratio (non-integer rate ratio) path (DSP.resample_filter's default is 32).\n\nt0 is preserved (see module docs: DSP.resample already compensates for the anti-aliasing filter's own group delay), and dt is set to exactly 1 / new_rate.\n\nReturns ts unchanged (a copy) if new_rate == sample_rate(ts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Resample.resample"},{"category":"section","location":"api/detectors/#Detectors-and-reference-curves","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"Detector geometry, antenna response, arrival-time delays, and the aLIGO/Advanced Virgo design- sensitivity curves.","title":"Detectors and reference curves"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.H1","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"H1 :: Detector\n\nLIGO Hanford Observatory's 4km interferometer (LALSuite's \"LHO4k\"). Geometry (vertex location, arm-direction unit vectors used to build response) ported verbatim from LALSuite's lalCachedDetectors table (`LALLHO4K*constants inlal/lib/tools/LALDetectors.h`) – see this file's module docstring for the exact source and provenance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.H1"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.L1","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"L1 :: Detector\n\nLIGO Livingston Observatory's 4km interferometer (LALSuite's \"LLO4k\"). Geometry ported verbatim from LALSuite's lalCachedDetectors table (`LALLLO4K*constants inlal/lib/tools/LALDetectors.h) -- see this file's module docstring for the exact source and provenance. See [H1`](@ref) for the general construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.L1"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.V1","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"V1 :: Detector\n\nVirgo Observatory's 3km interferometer (LALSuite's \"VIRGO\"). Geometry ported verbatim from LALSuite's lalCachedDetectors table (LAL_VIRGO_* constants in lal/lib/tools/LALDetectors.h) – see this file's module docstring for the exact source and provenance. See H1 for the general construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.V1"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.AbstractDetector","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"AbstractDetector\n\nSupertype of Detector (named, host-side) and DetectorGeometry (isbits, usable inside GPU kernels and Enzyme-differentiated code). Every detector function accepts either; geometry returns the underlying DetectorGeometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.AbstractDetector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.Detector","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"Detector(prefix, geometry::DetectorGeometry{Float64})\n\nA named interferometer: prefix is the two-character GWOSC/channel-name prefix (e.g. \"H1\"), geometry its DetectorGeometry. See H1/L1/V1 for the three ready-made instances this package provides, ported verbatim from LALSuite's lalCachedDetectors table (see this file's module docstring for the exact source).\n\nNot isbits (the prefix is a String): pass geometry(detector) into GPU kernels or Enzyme-differentiated code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.Detector"},{"category":"type","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.DetectorGeometry","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"DetectorGeometry{T}(location, response)\n\nA gravitational-wave interferometer's geometry: Earth-centered Cartesian location vector (meters) and response tensor (dimensionless, the d^{ab} of Anderson et al. PRD 63 042003 (2001), stored as response[i][j]). Plain bits, so it can be passed to or built inside GPU kernels.\n\nDetectorGeometry{Float32}(H1) converts a detector's geometry to another element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.DetectorGeometry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.antenna_response_gmst-Tuple{Detector, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"antenna_response_gmst(detector::AbstractDetector, ra, dec, psi, gmst) -> (Fplus, Fcross)\n\ncompute_antenna_response with the Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (radians) given instead of the GPS time. Allocation-free and type-generic (the result's type follows the promoted argument and geometry types), so it is safe inside GPU kernels and Enzyme-differentiated code when called on a DetectorGeometry.\n\ngmst is not reduced modulo 2pi (it is ~4e4 rad for current GPS times): pass Float32(mod2pi(gmst(gps_time))) for single-precision evaluation, or the hour angle loses ~1e-3 rad.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.antenna_response_gmst"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.compute_antenna_response-Tuple{AbstractDetector, Vararg{Real, 4}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"compute_antenna_response(detector::AbstractDetector, ra, dec, psi, gps_time) -> (Fplus, Fcross)\n\nAntenna pattern functions F+/Fx for a gravitational wave from sky position (ra, dec) (equatorial right ascension/declination, radians), polarization angle psi (radians), arriving at gps_time (GPS seconds). Direct port of XLALComputeDetAMResponse (lal/lib/tools/DetResponse.c), implementing Eqs. (B4), (B5), (B7) of Anderson et al., PRD 63, 042003 (2001).\n\nComputes gmst(gps_time) (relatively expensive: leap-second table scan + Julian-day conversion) and calls antenna_response_gmst, which callers that already hold the GMST – and GPU kernels – should use directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.compute_antenna_response"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.geometry-Tuple{DetectorGeometry}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"geometry(detector::AbstractDetector) -> DetectorGeometry\n\nThe isbits DetectorGeometry of detector (itself, if it already is one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.geometry"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.time_delay_from_geocenter-Tuple{AbstractDetector, Real, Real, Real}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"time_delay_from_geocenter(detector::AbstractDetector, ra, dec, gps_time) -> Float64\n\nLight-travel-time offset (seconds) between the geocenter and detector, for a gravitational wave arriving from sky position (ra, dec) (equatorial right ascension/declination, radians) at gps_time (GPS seconds). Direct port of XLALTimeDelayFromEarthCenter (lal/lib/tools/TimeDelay.c), via its XLALArrivalTimeDiff helper specialized to detector2 = earth_center = (0,0,0).\n\nSign convention (matches LALSuite exactly): positive when the wavefront arrives at the detector after arriving at the geocenter – i.e. t_detector = t_geocenter + time_delay_from_geocenter(...). Computes gmst(gps_time) and calls time_delay_from_geocenter_gmst.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.time_delay_from_geocenter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.time_delay_from_geocenter_gmst-Tuple{Detector, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"time_delay_from_geocenter_gmst(detector::AbstractDetector, ra, dec, gmst) -> delay\n\ntime_delay_from_geocenter with the Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (radians) given instead of the GPS time. Allocation-free and type-generic, like antenna_response_gmst.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Detectors.time_delay_from_geocenter_gmst"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.aligo_design_asd-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"aligo_design_asd(f::AbstractVector{<:Real}) -> Vector{Float64}\naligo_design_asd([f0::Real,] df::Real, n::Integer) -> FrequencySeries\n\nAdvanced LIGO \"zero-detuning, high-power\" design-sensitivity amplitude spectral density (strain/sqrt(Hz)), sqrt.(aligo_design_psd(...)). See aligo_design_psd for the source and caveats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.aligo_design_asd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.aligo_design_psd-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"aligo_design_psd(f::AbstractVector{<:Real}) -> Vector{Float64}\naligo_design_psd([f0::Real,] df::Real, n::Integer) -> FrequencySeries\n\nAdvanced LIGO \"zero-detuning, high-power\" design-sensitivity power spectral density (strain^2/Hz), ported from LALSimulation's XLALSimNoisePSDaLIGOZeroDetHighPower (lalsimulation/lib/ LALSimNoisePSD.c) – see this file's module docstring for the exact formula, its provenance (Buonanno & Chen, Phys. Rev. D 64, 062006 (2001), quantum noise; plus a suspension/coating thermal -noise fit), and an important naming caveat (this is NOT the simpler closed-form \"Ajith fit\" some other codebases use under a similar name).\n\nValid above ~9 Hz (LALSimulation's own documented range for this model – it omits seismic/ Newtonian-noise contributions that dominate the design curve below that).\n\nSee the \"Public API\" note above this docstring for the DC-bin (f=0) convention used by the FrequencySeries-returning form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.aligo_design_psd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.avirgo_design_asd-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"avirgo_design_asd(f::AbstractVector{<:Real}) -> Vector{Float64}\navirgo_design_asd([f0::Real,] df::Real, n::Integer) -> FrequencySeries\n\nAdvanced Virgo design-sensitivity amplitude spectral density (strain/sqrt(Hz)), sqrt.(avirgo_design_psd(...)). See avirgo_design_psd for the source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.avirgo_design_asd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/detectors/#GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.avirgo_design_psd-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"Detectors & Reference Curves","text":"avirgo_design_psd(f::AbstractVector{<:Real}) -> Vector{Float64}\navirgo_design_psd([f0::Real,] df::Real, n::Integer) -> FrequencySeries\n\nAdvanced Virgo design-sensitivity power spectral density (strain^2/Hz), ported from LALSimulation's XLALSimNoisePSDAdvVirgo (lalsimulation/lib/LALSimNoisePSD.c): a phenomenological log-polynomial/exponential fit, Eq. 6 of Manzotti & Dietz 2012 (arXiv:1202.4031), to the AdvVirgo design curve published at http://wwwcascina.virgo.infin.it/advirgo. See this file's module docstring for further provenance detail.\n\nSee the \"Public API\" note above aligo_design_psd for the DC-bin (f=0) convention used by the FrequencySeries-returning form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.ReferenceCurves.avirgo_design_psd"},{"category":"section","location":"api/time/#Time-and-segments","page":"Time & Segments","text":"GPS/UTC conversion with leap seconds, Segment/SegmentList algebra, and Greenwich mean sidereal time.","title":"Time and segments"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.GPS_EPOCH","page":"Time & Segments","text":"GPS_EPOCH :: DateTime\n\nGPS time's epoch: 1980-01-06T00:00:00 UTC (the start of GPS week 0). By definition, GPS time equals UTC at this instant (GPS - UTC = 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.GPS_EPOCH"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.gps_to_utc-Tuple{Real}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"gps_to_utc(gps::Real) -> DateTime\n\nConvert GPS seconds (elapsed SI seconds since GPS_EPOCH) to a UTC date/time, leap-second aware via LEAP_SECOND_TABLE. Rounds to the nearest millisecond (DateTime's resolution limit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.gps_to_utc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.leap_seconds-Tuple{Real}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"leap_seconds(gps_or_utc) -> Int\n\nGPS - UTC in whole seconds, as of the given instant (either a GPS time in seconds, Real, or a UTC DateTime). This is 0 at GPS_EPOCH and increases by 1 at each historical leap-second insertion (see LEAP_SECOND_TABLE).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.leap_seconds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.utc_to_gps-Tuple{Dates.DateTime}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"utc_to_gps(t::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nConvert a UTC date/time to GPS seconds (elapsed SI seconds since GPS_EPOCH), leap-second aware via LEAP_SECOND_TABLE. Sub-millisecond precision is not representable (DateTime's own resolution limit); this is not a concern for GW analysis timescales.\n\nCaveat shared by essentially every such converter: DateTime cannot represent the leap second itself (23:59:60) since Julia's Dates module has no notion of leap seconds – only ordinary instants strictly before or after an insertion are meaningful inputs here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.GPSTime.utc_to_gps"},{"category":"type","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Segments.Segment","page":"Time & Segments","text":"Segment(start, stop)\n\nA half-open GPS-time interval [start, stop), start < stop required. Same half-open convention TimeSeries/FrequencySeries use for crop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Segments.Segment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Segments.SegmentList","page":"Time & Segments","text":"SegmentList(segments)\nSegmentList()\n\nA sorted, coalesced list of Segments: constructing from any iterable of Segments sorts by start time and merges overlapping-or-touching segments into one, as a maintained invariant (so every SegmentList a caller holds is always already in canonical form – no separate \"did you remember to coalesce?\" step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Segments.SegmentList"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#Base.intersect-Tuple{SegmentList, SegmentList}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"intersect(a::SegmentList, b::SegmentList) -> SegmentList\n\nTime covered by both lists. Implemented as a linear two-pointer sweep over each (sorted, disjoint) list's segments, since both inputs are already in canonical form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.intersect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#Base.union-Tuple{SegmentList, SegmentList}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"union(a::SegmentList, b::SegmentList) -> SegmentList\n\nAll time covered by either list, coalesced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.union"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Segments.coalesce_segments-Tuple{Any}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"coalesce_segments(segments) -> SegmentList\n\nSort segments (any iterable of Segment) and merge overlapping-or-touching ones. Equivalent to SegmentList(segments) – provided as an explicitly-named entry point since \"coalescing\" is a named operation callers reach for directly (e.g. after collecting raw candidate segments from a DQ-bitmask scan).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Segments.coalesce_segments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Segments.gaps-Tuple{SegmentList}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"gaps(sl::SegmentList) -> SegmentList\ngaps(sl::SegmentList, bound::Segment) -> SegmentList\n\nThe complement of sl: time NOT covered by any of its segments.\n\nWithout bound, only the \"internal\" gaps strictly between sl's first and last segment are returned (there is no well-defined gap before the first or after the last segment when the list's own extent is the only reference frame).\n\nWith an explicit bound, the complement is computed within [bound.start, bound.stop) – e.g. gaps(good_data_segments, Segment(analysis_start, analysis_end)) to find the vetoed time within a specific analysis window, including any leading/trailing gap against that window's edges. sl's segments are clipped to bound first (segments partially or fully outside bound are handled correctly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Segments.gaps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Segments.overlaps-Tuple{Segment, Segment}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"overlaps(a::Segment, b::Segment) -> Bool\n\nWhether the two half-open intervals share any point. Merely touching (a.stop == b.start) does NOT count as overlapping here (no shared point in a half-open interval) – but touching segments are still merged by coalesce_segments/SegmentList, which is the more permissive, canonicalizing notion of \"adjacent enough to combine\" that segment-list libraries conventionally use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Segments.overlaps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration-Tuple{SegmentList}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"duration(sl::SegmentList) -> Float64\n\nTotal covered time, i.e. the sum of each (disjoint, by construction) segment's duration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration-Tuple{Segment}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"duration(s::Segment) -> Float64\n\nLength of the interval, s.stop - s.start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.Types.duration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.UTCTime","page":"Time & Segments","text":"UTCTime\n\nBroken-down UTC civil time, mirroring the fields of XLALGPSToUTC's C struct tm output that gmst's Julian-day conversion actually needs (year, month, day, hour, minute, second). sec can be 60 for a UTC leap second, matching LALSuite's convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.UTCTime"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._convert_civil_time_to_jd-Tuple{GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.UTCTime}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"_convert_civil_time_to_jd(utc::UTCTime) -> Float64\n\nPort of XLALConvertCivilTimeToJD (lal/lib/date/XLALCivilTime.c) – the \"short\" Julian Day formula valid for dates since 1900 March (LALSuite's own comment: not expected to be used before that). Julian days start at noon, hence the - 0.5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._convert_civil_time_to_jd"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._delta_tai_utc-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"_delta_tai_utc(gpssec::Integer) -> Int\n\nPort of delta_tai_utc (lal/lib/date/XLALCivilTime.c): the change in TAI-UTC exactly AT gpssec relative to gpssec - 1 (nonzero only in the single GPS second a leap second is inserted/removed; 0 otherwise). Used to detect whether UTC needs a 60th (\"leap\") second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._delta_tai_utc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._leap_seconds_tai_utc-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"_leap_seconds_tai_utc(gpssec::Integer) -> Int\n\nPort of XLALLeapSeconds (lal/lib/date/XLALCivilTime.c): returns TAI-UTC (in seconds) in effect at the given integer GPS second, by scanning the module's LEAPS table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime._leap_seconds_tai_utc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.gmst-Tuple{Real}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"gmst(gps_seconds::Real) -> Float64\n\nGreenwich Mean Sidereal Time, in radians, at the given GPS time. Direct port of XLALGreenwichMeanSiderealTime/XLALGreenwichSiderealTime (with equation_of_equinoxes = 0) from lal/lib/date/XLALSiderealTime.c – see this file's module docstring for why a generic astronomy package is deliberately NOT used here.\n\nThe result is NOT reduced modulo 2pi (matching LALSuite's own convention – callers that need a value in `[0, 2pi)should reduce it themselves, e.g. viamod2pi`).\n\ngps_seconds may carry a fractional part (sub-second precision is handled the same way LALSuite's LIGOTimeGPS integer-seconds/nanoseconds split is: the integer part feeds the UTC/leap-second/Julian-day machinery which only has whole-second resolution, and the fractional part is added back in as t_lo).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.gmst"},{"category":"method","location":"api/time/#GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.gps_to_utc_seconds-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Time & Segments","text":"gps_to_utc_seconds(gpssec::Integer) -> UTCTime\n\nPort of XLALGPSToUTC (lal/lib/date/XLALCivilTime.c): converts an integer GPS second count to a broken-down UTC civil time, accounting for leap seconds via the module's LEAPS table.\n\nLALSuite computes this via unixsec = gpssec - leapsec + XLAL_EPOCH_GPS_TAI_UTC + XLAL_EPOCH_UNIX_GPS fed to gmtime_r; Julia's Dates provides the equivalent Unix-epoch -> civil-calendar conversion (proleptic Gregorian, matching glibc's gmtime_r in the range GW data covers) via Dates.unix2datetime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GravitationalWaveData.SiderealTime.gps_to_utc_seconds"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/03_filtering_whitening/#Filtering-and-Whitening","page":"Filtering & Whitening","text":"Real gravitational-wave strain data is, in raw form, dominated by low-frequency seismic and suspension noise – many orders of magnitude louder than any astrophysical signal buried in the band. Before a transient like a binary black hole merger becomes visible by eye, the data needs conditioning: band-limiting filters to cut out noise outside the band of interest, notch filters to remove narrow contaminating spectral lines (mains power and its harmonics), and whitening to flatten the spectrum so no single frequency band dominates the visual scale.\n\nThis tutorial walks through bandpass/highpass/lowpass/ notch, then whiten – inverse-spectrum-truncation whitening (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md's \"Whitening\" section, also on this site under Conventions, for the full algorithm writeup) – and finishes by combining bandpass and whitening to reveal the classic GW150914 \"chirp\".\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Filtering & Whitening"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/03_filtering_whitening/#Fetching-real-strain-data","page":"Filtering & Whitening","text":"We use the same standard 4096 s off-source segment covering GW150914 that the rest of this project's tutorials and tests use, fetched live from GWOSC via fetch_open_data (cached locally under ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain on repeat calls, so re-running this tutorial doesn't re-download the file).\n\ngps_start, gps_end = 1126256640, 1126260736\ngps_merger = 1126259462.4   # GW150914 merger GPS time\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", gps_start, gps_end)\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\nA raw zoom around the merger shows nothing obviously astrophysical: the signal is many orders of magnitude smaller than the low-frequency noise dominating the plot's vertical scale (note the 1e-19-ish amplitude axis – the actual GW150914 strain is close to 1e-21).\n\nraw_rel = collect(times(strain)) .- gps_merger\nraw_mask = abs.(raw_rel) .<= 0.5\nplot(raw_rel[raw_mask], strain.data[raw_mask];\n     xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Amplitude (strain)\", label=\"H1 raw strain\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Fetching real strain data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/03_filtering_whitening/#Band-limiting-and-notch-filters","page":"Filtering & Whitening","text":"bandpass, highpass, lowpass, and notch are all zero-phase Butterworth filters (DSP.digitalfilter + DSP.filtfilt, fixed order=4 by default – see src/GravitationalWaveData/Filtering.jl's header comment for why the order is fixed rather than auto-selected). We work on a 16 s segment centered on the merger, long enough that filtfilt's edge transients don't touch the region we plot.\n\nseg = crop(strain, gps_merger - 8, gps_merger + 8)\nseglen = round(Int, 4 * sample_rate(seg))   # 4 s Welch segments for the ASD estimates below\n16384\n\nhighpass/lowpass roll off everything below/above a single corner frequency; overlaying their ASDs against the raw segment's shows exactly where each starts cutting:\n\nseg_asd = crop(asd(seg; seglen=seglen), 8.0, nothing)   # drop the f=0 bin (breaks log-log axes)\nhp_asd = crop(asd(highpass(seg, 30); seglen=seglen), 8.0, nothing)\nlp_asd = crop(asd(lowpass(seg, 500); seglen=seglen), 8.0, nothing)\n\nplot(seg_asd; label=\"raw\", xlim=(8, 2000))\nplot!(hp_asd; label=\"highpass(30 Hz)\")\nplot!(lp_asd; label=\"lowpass(500 Hz)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=3}\n\nnotch instead removes one narrow band – e.g. the 60 Hz US mains line, visible as a sharp spike in the raw ASD and cleanly excised after notching:\n\nnotched = notch(seg, 60; bandwidth=2.0)\nnotched_asd = crop(asd(notched; seglen=seglen), 8.0, nothing)\n\nplot(seg_asd; label=\"raw\", xlim=(50, 70))\nplot!(notched_asd; label=\"notch(60 Hz)\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}\n\nbandpass combines a high- and a low-pass corner in one filter. Applying bandpass(50, 250) together with notches at the first three 60 Hz mains harmonics – already turns up a spike right at the merger time, in an otherwise unremarkable-looking segment:\n\nconditioned = notch(notch(notch(seg, 60.0), 120.0), 180.0)\nconditioned = bandpass(conditioned, 50.0, 250.0)\n\ncond_zoom = crop(conditioned, gps_merger - 0.3, gps_merger + 0.3)\ncond_rel = collect(times(cond_zoom)) .- gps_merger\nplot(cond_rel, cond_zoom.data;\n     xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Amplitude (strain)\",\n     label=\"H1, 50-250 Hz bandpass + notches\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Band-limiting and notch filters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/03_filtering_whitening/#Whitening","page":"Filtering & Whitening","text":"Filtering alone only band-limits; it doesn't equalize the remaining power across frequency, so low-frequency noise inside the passband can still dominate the eye's attention. whiten instead performs inverse spectrum truncation against a supplied PSD/ASD: design a time-domain FIR filter approximating 1/ASD(f), taper/truncate it to fduration seconds, and convolve it with the data via the overlap-save method – never a naive FFT/divide/inverse-FFT, which would ring badly at segment edges. See docs/CONVENTIONS.md's \"Whitening\" section for the full algorithm writeup, and its Validation section for the measured agreement with a reference implementation.\n\nWe estimate the ASD from the very same 4096 s segment we're about to whiten – in a real analysis you'd typically use a separate off-source stretch, but GW150914's own signal is far too short (a fraction of a second) to meaningfully bias a multi-minute median-Welch estimate:\n\nasd_est = asd(strain; seglen=round(Int, 4 * sample_rate(strain)))\nwhite = whiten(strain, asd_est; highpass=20.0)\n\nwhite_rel = collect(times(strain)) .- gps_merger\nwhite_mask = abs.(white_rel) .<= 0.5\nplot(white_rel[white_mask], white.data[white_mask];\n     xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Whitened strain\", label=\"H1 whitened\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nOn stationary Gaussian noise, whiten normalizes to zero mean and unit variance – notice the vertical scale is now O(1) rather than O(1e-19). Per docs/CONVENTIONS.md's documented caveat, the first/last fduration/2 seconds (default fduration=2.0, so 1 s here) of the full whitened series are corrupted by the FIR filter's settle-in transient; since the 1 s window we plotted above sits deep in the interior of the 4096 s segment we whitened, that doesn't affect anything shown here, but it is worth cropping off explicitly whenever a whitened segment's own start/end is close to data you care about.","title":"Whitening"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/03_filtering_whitening/#Combining-bandpass-and-whitening:-the-GW150914-chirp","page":"Filtering & Whitening","text":"Whitening alone already reveals broadband transients, but combining it with a bandpass around the detector's most sensitive band sharpens the signal further – the combination behind the canonical \"whitened GW150914\" figure:\n\nbp_white = bandpass(white, 30.0, 400.0)\nchirp_zoom = crop(bp_white, gps_merger - 0.15, gps_merger + 0.05)\n\nchirp_rel = collect(times(chirp_zoom)) .- gps_merger\nplot(chirp_rel, chirp_zoom.data;\n     xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Whitened strain\",\n     title=\"GW150914, H1 (30-400 Hz bandpass + whiten)\", legend=false, linewidth=1.5)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nThe characteristic gravitational-wave \"chirp\" – rising amplitude and frequency as the two black holes spiral together – is now visible by eye, peaking right around the merger GPS time (1126259462.4), in data that looked completely unremarkable in the very first plot above. This is the same idea behind Figure 1 of Abbott et al. (2016), the LIGO-Virgo discovery paper for GW150914; the 12_gw150914_capstone tutorial builds on exactly this filtering/whitening pipeline (adding a Q-transform) to reproduce that figure more fully.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Combining bandpass and whitening: the GW150914 chirp"},{"category":"section","location":"#GravitationalWaveData.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia toolkit for gravitational-wave detector data: TimeSeries/FrequencySeries/Spectrogram types, GWOSC and frame-file I/O, PSD/ASD estimation, filtering and whitening, resampling, spectrograms, Q-transforms, GPS time and segments, detector geometry and antenna response, and coherence, built on DSP.jl and FFTW.jl.","title":"GravitationalWaveData.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"New to the package? Start with Getting Started: loading real GWOSC strain data into a TimeSeries and making a first plot.\nLooking for a specific topic? Each tutorial in the sidebar covers one module, and the GW150914 capstone combines them to reproduce the discovery figure.\nWant the full function/type list? See the API Reference.\nWant the numerical conventions (PSD normalization, whitening algorithm, GPS/leap seconds, ...) and how they were validated? See Conventions.\nPrefer a notebook? Every tutorial is also available as a Jupyter notebook under notebooks/, and the Q-transform and filter-design topics have interactive explorers, as Pluto.jl notebooks under notebooks/pluto/ and standalone Bonito.jl web apps under notebooks/bonito/.","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"GravitationalWaveData\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#GravitationalWaveData.jl-conventions","page":"Conventions","text":"This page collects the non-obvious numerical and physical conventions GravitationalWaveData.jl commits to. Each is also documented, with its exact provenance, at its point of use in src/GravitationalWaveData/; when the two disagree, the source comment is authoritative.\n\nThe conventions follow established GW-analysis practice (scipy.signal, gwpy, LALSuite) rather than inventing new ones, so results are directly comparable with other pipelines. The measured agreement with those reference implementations is summarized under Validation.","title":"GravitationalWaveData.jl conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#PSD/ASD-normalization","page":"Conventions","text":"psd/asd (src/GravitationalWaveData/PSD.jl) estimate a one-sided power/amplitude spectral density from a TimeSeries using median-Welch averaging.\n\nSegmentation: the series is split into seglen-sample segments with fractional overlap (default 0.5), starting at 1-based indices 1, 1+step, 1+2*step, ... where step = round(seglen * (1 - overlap)). Too-short input raises ArgumentError rather than silently returning zero segments. csd/coherence (src/GravitationalWaveData/Coherence.jl) reuse this exact segmentation via the shared internal helper _welch_starts, so the two never drift apart.\nWindow: each segment is multiplied by a Hann window (DSP.Windows.hanning, the symmetric convention — endpoints exactly zero, not the periodic/DFT-even convention some FFT-adjacent code uses), i.e. 0.5 * (1 - cos(2*pi*n/(N-1))). This is also scipy.signal.welch's default.\nScaling: each windowed segment's periodogram is (2 * dt / seglen) * abs2(rfft(segment)) / win_power, where win_power = mean(window.^2) corrects for the window's power loss. This is the standard density normalization (scipy.signal.welch's scaling=\"density\"): ∫ psd(f) df over [0, Nyquist] approximates the time series' variance.\nDC/Nyquist bin-halving: the blanket 2 * factor above assumes every one-sided bin has a mirrored negative-frequency counterpart to fold in. The DC bin (always) and the Nyquist bin (only when seglen is even) have no such mirror, so they are divided by 2 immediately after — otherwise their power would be double-counted. This is a common, easy-to-miss detail in one-sided PSD estimators; get it wrong and low-frequency/Nyquist-adjacent bins are off by a factor of 2.\nAveraging across segments: method=:median (the default, the standard LIGO convention) takes the per-frequency-bin median periodogram across segments — far more robust to a handful of segments being contaminated by loud transient glitches than a mean would be — and then divides by ln(2) to debias it (the median of a chi-squared/exponential-distributed periodogram systematically underestimates the mean by very close to that factor, asymptotically exact for the segment counts this package sees in practice, e.g. O(500) segments for a 1024s off-source stretch at 4s/50% overlap). method=:mean takes the plain per-bin mean and skips the ln(2) correction entirely (it isn't needed for a mean estimator).\nasd is exactly sqrt.(psd(...)).\nUnits: PSD is strain²/Hz; ASD is strain/√Hz (or the equivalent unit² /Hz, unit/√Hz for a non-strain input series).","title":"PSD/ASD normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#CSD-/-coherence-normalization","page":"Conventions","text":"csd/coherence (src/GravitationalWaveData/Coherence.jl) generalize the same Welch machinery to a pair of channels, following scipy.signal.csd/scipy.signal.coherence:\n\nSame segmentation/window as psd (reusing _welch_starts/_welch_window directly), same DC/Nyquist bin-halving, same density scaling generalized to a cross term: Pxy(f) = mean_over_segments((2*dt/seglen) * conj(rfft(x)) .* rfft(y) / win_power).\nAlways mean-averaged, never median, because a median of complex values isn't the same well-defined robust statistic a median of real, non-negative PSD values is.\nCoherence is |Pxy(f)|^2 / (Pxx(f) * Pyy(f)), real-valued, in [0, 1], with Pxx/Pyy computed via psd(...; method=:mean) (mean, not median — so the denominator uses the same averaging convention as the numerator's Pxy) — matching scipy.signal.coherence's own average=\"mean\" default.\nDetrending gap, deliberately not closed: scipy.signal.csd/coherence detrend (subtract the per-segment mean) by default; this package's psd does not detrend, and csd/coherence follow suit so numerator and denominator use the same convention. The effect on real data is negligible (see Validation).","title":"CSD / coherence normalization"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Whitening","page":"Conventions","text":"whiten (src/GravitationalWaveData/Filtering.jl) whitens a TimeSeries against a supplied PSD/ASD using inverse spectrum truncation (the algorithm used by gwpy's TimeSeries.whiten()), not the naive \"FFT, divide by ASD, inverse FFT\" approach, which rings badly at segment edges.\n\nThe algorithm, in order:\n\nInterpolate the supplied PSD/ASD (linear interpolation) onto the frequency grid with spacing 1/duration(ts) — so a single off-source ASD estimate can be reused to whiten several different on-source segments without needing to already match their exact resolution.\nDesign a time-domain FIR filter approximating 1/ASD(f): transform to the time domain, truncate to fduration seconds (default 2s) of taps, and taper the truncation with a window (default Hann) so the filter doesn't have unbounded support or a sharp cutoff discontinuity. If highpass is given, the filter's response below that frequency is hard-zeroed first.\nConvolve that FIR filter with the mean-detrended input data using the overlap-save method, with the input's own edges tapered — never a bare frequency-domain divide.\nScale the result by sqrt(2 * dt): on stationary Gaussian input, whitened output has zero mean and unit variance.\n\nEdge effects: a segment of length fduration/2 at each end of the output is corrupted by the FIR filter's settle-in transient (tapered rather than exact). Crop it off if that matters downstream.\n\nBackground theory: arXiv:gr-qc/0509116.","title":"Whitening"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Filtering","page":"Conventions","text":"bandpass/highpass/lowpass/notch (src/GravitationalWaveData/Filtering.jl) use a fixed-order (default 4) Butterworth design applied zero-phase via DSP.filtfilt. Filter order is explicit, not auto-selected from a ripple/attenuation specification, so results differ from tools that design the order automatically (for example gwpy's iirdesign-based .bandpass()).","title":"Filtering"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#GPS-time","page":"Conventions","text":"GW data is timestamped in GPS seconds: a continuous SI-second count from the GPS epoch (1980-01-06T00:00:00 UTC) with no leap seconds inserted after that epoch — GPS time never stops, skips, or repeats a second. This is why GW pipelines universally prefer it over UTC as an internal timestamp: it's monotonic and gap-free.\n\ngps_to_utc/utc_to_gps (src/GravitationalWaveData/GPSTime.jl) convert between GPS seconds and UTC DateTimes, leap-second aware via a historical table (LEAP_SECOND_TABLE) sourced from IERS Bulletin C, current through Bulletin C 71 (July 2025; most recent actual leap second inserted 2016-12-31, i.e. GPS - UTC = 18s from 2017-01-01 onward). This is a real table lookup, not a single hardcoded offset constant — a hardcoded constant silently goes stale the next time IERS announces a new leap second, and there is no way to detect that staleness at runtime. If IERS announces a new leap second (Bulletin C is issued twice yearly), extend LEAP_SECOND_TABLE with the new entry; both conversion functions derive everything from that one table, so nothing else needs to change.\n\nA related but independently-sourced leap-second table lives in src/GravitationalWaveData/SiderealTime.jl (LEAPS), ported verbatim from LALSuite's own XLALLeapSeconds.h rather than IERS directly — this is deliberate: gmst (Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time) must match LALSuite's exact numerical convention bit-for-bit for interoperability with LALSuite-based pipelines, which means reproducing LALSuite's specific leap-second bookkeeping rather than a more astronomically \"correct\" modern one. See that module's header comment for the full rationale. The two tables agree on every leap second either one is aware of; they are kept as two separate tables because they serve two different faithfulness goals (general UTC correctness vs. LALSuite bit-for-bit reproduction), not out of oversight.\n\nCaveat shared by essentially every GPS/UTC converter: Dates.DateTime cannot represent the leap second itself (23:59:60), since Julia's Dates module has no notion of leap seconds — only ordinary instants strictly before or after an insertion are meaningful inputs to utc_to_gps.","title":"GPS time"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Segment-/-SegmentList:-half-open-intervals","page":"Conventions","text":"Segment/SegmentList (src/GravitationalWaveData/Segments.jl) represent GPS-time intervals for data-quality vetoing, following the standard LIGO segment-list convention:\n\nEvery interval is half-open, [start, stop) — start is included, stop is not. This is the same half-open convention TimeSeries.crop/FrequencySeries.crop use (see below), kept consistent across the package so a boundary sample is never double-counted or dropped depending on which API touched it.\nA SegmentList is always sorted and coalesced (overlapping-or-touching segments merged) as a maintained class invariant — every SegmentList a caller holds is already in canonical form, no separate \"did you remember to coalesce?\" step needed before, say, calling union/intersect.\nTouching segments merge, but don't \"overlap\": overlaps(a, b) is false when a.stop == b.start (no shared point in a half-open interval), but coalesce_segments (and SegmentList construction) still merges such touching segments — the more permissive \"adjacent enough to combine\" notion segment-list libraries conventionally use for canonicalization.\ngaps(sl) returns the complement of sl; without an explicit bound::Segment, only internal gaps (strictly between the first and last segment) are meaningful, since there's no well-defined gap before/after the list's own extent otherwise. Pass bound to compute the complement within a specific analysis window, including any leading/trailing gap against that window's edges.","title":"Segment / SegmentList: half-open intervals"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#TimeSeries-/-FrequencySeries-/-Spectrogram:-axes-and-arithmetic","page":"Conventions","text":"TimeSeries: sample k (1-based) is at GPS time t0 + (k-1)*dt. FrequencySeries: bin k (1-based) is at frequency f0 + (k-1)*df (f0 defaults to 0.0). Spectrogram: stores both axis pairs at once — data is an nfreq x ntime matrix (row = frequency bin, column = time bin), the same (freq, time) order DSP.spectrogram's power field uses. (Some other tools store (time, freq); transpose when exchanging data with them.) A Spectrogram's t0 is the GPS time of the center of its first STFT segment (not the start of the input TimeSeries), matching how DSP.spectrogram labels its time bins.\ncrop (on TimeSeries/FrequencySeries) restricts to a half-open [start, end) window, snapping each bound to the nearest sample/bin — the same half-open convention Segment uses. Either bound may be omitted (nothing) to mean \"start of the series\"/\"end of the series\".\nArithmetic (+ - * /) between two series is only defined when they share the exact same sample grid: same dt/t0/length for TimeSeries, same df/f0/length for FrequencySeries. A mismatch raises a specific ArgumentError naming exactly which of the three checks (rate/epoch/length) failed, rather than either an opaque bare DimensionMismatch (length-only) or, worse, silently broadcasting/truncating across a sampling-rate mismatch that would make the bin-by-bin correspondence meaningless. A series combined with a plain Number is unaffected by this check (broadcasts elementwise as usual).","title":"TimeSeries / FrequencySeries / Spectrogram: axes and arithmetic"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Q-transform","page":"Conventions","text":"q_transform/q_scan (src/GravitationalWaveData/QTransform.jl) implement the Omega-pipeline constant-Q transform (Chatterji 2005), with the tiling, bi-square window normalization and index conventions of gwpy's gwpy.signal.qtransform:\n\nInput is not whitened. q_transform FFTs its input with normalized_fft (rfft(data) / N, one-sided) and tiles that directly. For a whitened Q-scan, call whiten first and pass the result.\nMulti-Q search keeps one plane. Q values are log-spaced across qrange at the given mismatch, and the result is the single plane containing the loudest tile, not a merge across planes.\nRows have their own time grids. Each QRow in a QScan has its own t0/dt (higher frequencies get finer time resolution). No spline re-gridding onto a common time/frequency grid is done; interpolate yourself for display (see the Q-transform tutorial).\nEnergy normalization: each row's energy is divided by its median (norm=:median, default) or mean (norm=:mean), and snr = sqrt(2 * energy).\nNot implemented: false-alarm-rate estimation.","title":"Q-transform"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Reference-design-curves-(aLIGO-/-AdvVirgo)","page":"Conventions","text":"aligo_design_psd/aligo_design_asd/avirgo_design_psd/avirgo_design_asd (src/GravitationalWaveData/ReferenceCurves.jl) are ported verbatim from LALSimulation's LALSimNoisePSD.c, not re-derived. One naming trap worth flagging for anyone extending this module: the well-known simple closed-form \"Ajith fit\" some other codebases call aLIGOZeroDetHighPower is not what LALSimulation's function of the same name actually computes — the real function composes a full Buonanno & Chen (2001) quantum-noise model with a suspension/coating thermal-noise fit. This module ports that actual composition, since that's what it's cross-validated against. In the FrequencySeries-returning form, the DC bin (f=0) is set to exactly 0.0 rather than evaluated (both underlying formulas are singular there) — matching LALSimulation's own XLALSimNoisePSD convention; the plain-vector form has no such guard and will return whatever IEEE754 NaN/Inf the raw formula produces at f=0, same as calling the LALSimulation function directly would.","title":"Reference design curves (aLIGO / AdvVirgo)"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Further-reading","page":"Conventions","text":"Every convention above is documented in more depth, with exact formulas and full source citations, at its point of use: src/GravitationalWaveData/PSD.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/Coherence.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/Filtering.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/GPSTime.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/SiderealTime.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/Segments.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/Types.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/Spectrogram.jl, src/GravitationalWaveData/QTransform.jl, and src/GravitationalWaveData/ReferenceCurves.jl.","title":"Further reading"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Validation","page":"Conventions","text":"Each module was cross-checked numerically against an established reference implementation on the same inputs, using real GW150914 data wherever relevant. The comparison scripts live in examples/validation/ (see its README for how to run them; they need Linux or macOS, since LALSuite has no Windows build). The spot values they produced are kept as regression tests under test/.\n\nFunction(s) Reference Agreement\npsd/asd gwpy .psd() (median-Welch), 1024 s H1 off-source median rel. diff 0.14%, max 1.14%\ncsd/coherence scipy.signal.csd/coherence via gwpy, H1/L1 off-source CSD median rel. diff 0.0093%, max 0.70%; coherence abs. diff max 2.3e-5\nwhiten gwpy .whiten() interior rel. RMS diff 4.0e-12\nlowpass/highpass gwpy Butterworth filters rel. RMS diff 0.03% / ~2%\nbandpass gwpy .bandpass() ~24% rel. RMS diff, from gwpy auto-selecting a higher filter order (see Filtering)\nresample gwpy .resample(), 4096 → 2048 Hz median rel. PSD diff 0.34%, p95 0.56%\nspectrogram gwpy .spectrogram2() median rel. diff 0.35%\nq_transform/qscan_peak gwpy q_transform, 4 s H1 around GW150914 peak time/frequency/Q exact; energy/SNR rel. diff ~2e-8 (gwpy stores these as Float32)\nQ-tile internals gwpy QTile window values, indices and single-tile energy bit-for-bit\nload_gwf_strain load_gwosc_strain on the matching HDF5 file bit-for-bit\ngmst lal.GreenwichMeanSiderealTime bit-for-bit\ncompute_antenna_response lal.ComputeDetAMResponse max rel. diff ~1.2e-6\ntime_delay_from_geocenter lal.TimeDelayFromEarthCenter bit-for-bit\naligo_design_*/avirgo_design_* lalsimulation.SimNoisePSD* max rel. diff ~3e-15\ngps_to_utc gwpy.time.tconvert exact","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/12_gw150914_capstone/#Capstone:-GW150914","page":"Capstone: GW150914","text":"Every earlier tutorial in this site builds one piece of a gravitational-wave detection pipeline in isolation: Filtering & Whitening turns noise-dominated raw strain into a visible \"chirp\", and Q-transform turns the same data into the time-frequency track that is the single most recognizable image in gravitational-wave astronomy. This capstone combines both, end to end, on real two-detector data around GW150914 to reproduce, as closely as this package's current API allows, Figure 1 of Abbott et al. (2016), the LIGO-Virgo discovery paper: a whitened, bandpassed strain comparison between LIGO-Hanford (H1) and LIGO-Livingston (L1), stacked above each detector's own Q-transform of the same event.\n\nThis page assumes the Filtering & Whitening and Q-transform tutorials for the why and how of each individual step – bandpass/whiten's inverse-spectrum-truncation algorithm, q_transform's multi-Q search – and only briefly recaps them here. The payoff of this page is entirely in the combination.\n\nusing GravitationalWaveData\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()","title":"Capstone: GW150914"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/12_gw150914_capstone/#Fetching-two-detector-data","page":"Capstone: GW150914","text":"The same standard 4096 s off-source segment covering GW150914, this time for both LIGO sites, fetched live from GWOSC via fetch_open_data (cached locally under ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain – both files are already cached from earlier tutorials in this docs build, notably Coherence & CSD, which also uses this H1/L1 pair):\n\ngps_start, gps_end = 1126256640, 1126260736\ngps_merger = 1126259462.4   # GW150914 merger GPS time\n\nh1_path = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", gps_start, gps_end)\nl1_path = fetch_open_data(\"L1\", gps_start, gps_end)\nh1_full, _ = load_gwosc_strain(h1_path)\nl1_full, _ = load_gwosc_strain(l1_path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\nAs the Coherence & CSD tutorial and docs/CONVENTIONS.md both note, this particular L1 file has a real NaN gap in its first ~190 s (pre-lock data, well before GW150914). whiten mean-detrends its entire input in one pass – a single NaN anywhere in that input poisons the mean, and hence every output sample, not just the samples near the gap. GW150914's merger sits about 2822 s into this segment, far past the gap itself, so the fix is simply to crop down to a window around the merger before whitening, rather than whitening the full 4096 s series the way the single-detector Filtering & Whitening tutorial does for H1 (whose file has no such gap). ASD estimation is unaffected: asd's median-Welch averaging (see docs/CONVENTIONS.md) only needs more than half of a given frequency bin's segments to be finite to return a clean result, and the gap contaminates well under 5% of segments here, so it's safe to estimate each detector's ASD from the full raw file:\n\nseglen = round(Int, 4 * sample_rate(h1_full))   # 4 s Welch segments, matching 03/09's convention\nh1_asd = asd(h1_full; seglen=seglen)\nl1_asd = asd(l1_full; seglen=seglen)\nFrequencySeries{Float64}(8193 bins, f0=0.0, df=0.25, name=\"strain ASD\")\n\nNow crop both detectors to the same 16 s window around the merger – the same window Q-transform uses – which conveniently sits nowhere near L1's gap:\n\nseg_pad = 8.0\nh1_seg = crop(h1_full, gps_merger - seg_pad, gps_merger + seg_pad)\nl1_seg = crop(l1_full, gps_merger - seg_pad, gps_merger + seg_pad)\n\ncount(isnan, h1_seg.data), count(isnan, l1_seg.data)   # (0, 0) -- confirms the crop cleared the gap\n(0, 0)","title":"Fetching two-detector data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/12_gw150914_capstone/#Whitening-and-bandpassing:-the-chirp,-in-both-detectors","page":"Capstone: GW150914","text":"whiten against each detector's own ASD, then bandpass to the same 30-400 Hz band the Filtering & Whitening tutorial settles on for H1 – the combination behind the top panel of Figure 1 in Abbott et al. (2016):\n\nh1_white = whiten(h1_seg, h1_asd; highpass=20.0)\nl1_white = whiten(l1_seg, l1_asd; highpass=20.0)\n\nh1_chirp = bandpass(h1_white, 30.0, 400.0)\nl1_chirp = bandpass(l1_white, 30.0, 400.0)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(65536 samples, t0=1.1262594543999023e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=16.0s, name=\"strain\")\n\nComparing the two detectors directly needs one more correction. As Abbott et al. (2016) reports, GW150914 arrived at the two sites about 6.9 ms apart, and the detectors' relative orientation on Earth means one channel's signal is inverted relative to the other. The standard figure applies this correction (shift L1 by 6.9 ms and flip its sign) purely to overlay the two traces on one time axis for the figure – it has no effect on either detector's own data. GravitationalWaveData.jl has no shift method yet, but a TimeSeries is just data/t0/dt/name, so the same correction is one direct construction:\n\nl1_aligned = TimeSeries(-1 .* l1_chirp.data, l1_chirp.t0 + 0.0069, l1_chirp.dt; name=l1_chirp.name)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(65536 samples, t0=1.1262594544068024e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=16.0s, name=\"strain\")","title":"Whitening and bandpassing: the chirp, in both detectors"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/12_gw150914_capstone/#Q-transforms:-the-chirp,-in-time-frequency","page":"Capstone: GW150914","text":"q_transform works on the un-whitened 16 s crop directly (it computes its own normalized FFT internally – see Q-transform for why whitening isn't wired into it), restricted to the same 20-500 Hz band and merger-straddling peak search that tutorial uses, run once per detector:\n\nh1_scan, h1_peak = q_transform(h1_seg; frange=(20.0, 500.0),\n                                search=(gps_merger - 0.2, gps_merger + 0.1))\nl1_scan, l1_peak = q_transform(l1_seg; frange=(20.0, 500.0),\n                                search=(gps_merger - 0.2, gps_merger + 0.1))\n(h1_peak.time - gps_merger, h1_peak.frequency, h1_peak.q), (l1_peak.time - gps_merger, l1_peak.frequency, l1_peak.q)\n((0.02138662338256836, 140.3125, 5.65685424949238), (0.01162099838256836, 118.4375, 5.65685424949238))\n\nEach detector's Q-search independently locks onto a tile within milliseconds of the known merger time, at a similar Q – a good cross-detector sanity check that this isn't a single-channel fluke. The peak times aren't expected to reproduce the literature's 6.9 ms inter-site delay to high precision here (each Q-plane row has its own, fairly coarse, time sampling – see QScan's docstring), but they land in the same few-millisecond ballpark:\n\nabs(h1_peak.time - l1_peak.time)\n0.009765625\n\nAs in Q-transform, scan.rows holds each Q-plane's raw energy grid at its own per-row time resolution; the same small linear-interpolation helper from that tutorial resamples each detector's rows onto one shared time grid for a heatmap:\n\nfunction _lininterp(xs, ys, x)\n    x <= xs[1] && return ys[1]\n    x >= xs[end] && return ys[end]\n    i = searchsortedlast(xs, x)\n    x0, x1 = xs[i], xs[i + 1]\n    y0, y1 = ys[i], ys[i + 1]\n    return y0 + (y1 - y0) * (x - x0) / (x1 - x0)\nend\n\nfunction qscan_grid(scan, tlo, thi; ntime=400)\n    common_times = range(tlo, thi; length=ntime)\n    freqs = [row.frequency for row in scan.rows]\n    z = Array{Float64}(undef, length(scan.rows), ntime)\n    for (i, row) in enumerate(scan.rows)\n        n = length(row.energy)\n        row_times = row.t0 .+ row.dt .* (0:(n - 1))\n        z[i, :] .= [_lininterp(row_times, row.energy, t) for t in common_times]\n    end\n    return collect(common_times), freqs, z\nend\n\nzoom_lo, zoom_hi = gps_merger - 0.15, gps_merger + 0.05\n\nh1_grid_times, h1_freqs, h1_energy = qscan_grid(h1_scan, zoom_lo, zoom_hi)\nl1_grid_times, l1_freqs, l1_energy = qscan_grid(l1_scan, zoom_lo, zoom_hi)\nh1_grid_rel = h1_grid_times .- gps_merger\nl1_grid_rel = l1_grid_times .- gps_merger;","title":"Q-transforms: the chirp, in time-frequency"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/12_gw150914_capstone/#The-combined-figure","page":"Capstone: GW150914","text":"Three panels, each built as its own Plot object and suppressed with a trailing ; so it isn't displayed on its own (per the Documenter @example-block duplicate-render trap – see the Plotting Recipes tutorial), then composed with layout=(3, 1): the whitened-and-bandpassed time-domain chirp for both detectors on top, each detector's own Q-transform time-frequency track below it – echoing Figure 1 of Abbott et al. (2016).\n\nh1_zoom = crop(h1_chirp, zoom_lo, zoom_hi)\nl1_zoom = crop(l1_aligned, zoom_lo, zoom_hi)\nh1_zoom_rel = collect(times(h1_zoom)) .- gps_merger\nl1_zoom_rel = collect(times(l1_zoom)) .- gps_merger\n\np_chirp = plot(h1_zoom_rel, h1_zoom.data;\n               xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Whitened strain\", label=\"H1\",\n               color=:black, linewidth=1.5,\n               title=\"GW150914: whitened + bandpassed strain (30-400 Hz)\");\nplot!(p_chirp, l1_zoom_rel, l1_zoom.data;\n      xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Whitened strain\",\n      label=\"L1 (shifted 6.9 ms, inverted)\", color=:red, linewidth=1.5);\n\np_h1q = heatmap(h1_grid_rel, h1_freqs, h1_energy;\n                 xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", yscale=:log10,\n                 colorbar_title=\"Normalized energy\", color=:viridis,\n                 title=\"H1 Q-transform (Q=$(round(h1_peak.q; digits=2)))\");\n\np_l1q = heatmap(l1_grid_rel, l1_freqs, l1_energy;\n                 xlabel=\"Time from merger (s)\", ylabel=\"Frequency (Hz)\", yscale=:log10,\n                 colorbar_title=\"Normalized energy\", color=:viridis,\n                 title=\"L1 Q-transform (Q=$(round(l1_peak.q; digits=2)))\");\n\nplot(p_chirp, p_h1q, p_l1q; layout=(3, 1), size=(750, 950))\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=4}\n\nThe top panel shows the same rising-amplitude, rising-frequency \"chirp\" the Filtering & Whitening tutorial found in H1 alone, now visibly consistent between two independent, geographically separated detectors once L1's arrival-time offset and inverted orientation are accounted for. The two bottom panels show the same event as an upward-sweeping time-frequency track in each detector's own Q-transform, peaking within milliseconds of the same merger time found in the time domain. Two independent detectors, independently analyzed with two independent techniques (whitened time-domain filtering and a constant-Q time-frequency transform), agreeing on the same transient at the same GPS time is exactly the kind of consistency check that turned GW150914 from \"an interesting blip\" into the first direct detection of gravitational waves.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"The combined figure"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"Fetch a real detector-strain segment from the public GWOSC archive, load it as a TimeSeries (a uniformly-sampled, GPS-time-anchored series with crop/arithmetic/plotting attached), and make a first plot.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"GravitationalWaveData.jl is registered in Julia's General registry:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"GravitationalWaveData\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Loading-the-package","page":"Getting Started","text":"using GravitationalWaveData","title":"Loading the package"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Fetching-real-GW150914-strain-data","page":"Getting Started","text":"fetch_open_data downloads an official GWOSC science-run strain file covering a given GPS interval for one detector, and returns a local file path. GWOSC's public archive is split into fixed-length (typically 4096 s) segment files, so a request must fall entirely within one such file. Here we grab the standard H1 segment containing the first-ever gravitational-wave detection, GW150914 (merger GPS time ≈ 1126259462.4):\n\npath = fetch_open_data(\"H1\", 1126256640, 1126256640 + 4096)\n\"/home/runner/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain/H-H1_LOSC_4_V1-1126256640-4096.hdf5\"\n\nThe very first call downloads the file (a few hundred MB) into a local cache, by default ~/.cache/gravitationalwavedata/strain; every subsequent call for the same detector/GPS range/sample rate is served straight from that cache without touching the network again, so re-running this tutorial (or any other tutorial that reuses this same segment) is cheap after the first fetch.","title":"Fetching real GW150914 strain data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Loading-the-file-into-a-TimeSeries","page":"Getting Started","text":"load_gwosc_strain parses the GWOSC HDF5 file's /strain/Strain dataset (plus its per-second data-quality bitmask) into two TimeSeries: the strain itself, and a dq_mask series flagging data-quality issues second-by-second. This tutorial only uses the strain; see the GPS time & Segments tutorial for dq_mask/SegmentList usage.\n\nstrain, dq_mask = load_gwosc_strain(path)\n(TimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\"), TimeSeries{UInt32}(4096 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=1.0, duration=4096.0s, name=\"DQmask\"))\n\nstrain\nTimeSeries{Float64}(16777216 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=4096.0s, name=\"strain\")","title":"Loading the file into a TimeSeries"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Inspecting-the-TimeSeries","page":"Getting Started","text":"A TimeSeries is a uniformly-sampled series anchored to a GPS epoch: sample k (1-based) occurs at GPS time t0 + (k-1)*dt. A handful of accessors describe its sample grid:\n\nduration(strain)   # seconds\n4096.0\n\nsample_rate(strain)   # Hz\n4096.0\n\n# `times` returns the GPS time of every sample, as a range (not materialized as a Vector)\ntimes(strain)\n1.12625664e9:0.000244140625:1.1262607359997559e9","title":"Inspecting the TimeSeries"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Indexing-and-cropping","page":"Getting Started","text":"Integer indexing returns a single raw sample value; range indexing returns a new TimeSeries with its t0 shifted to match:\n\nstrain[1]\n7.881839997221286e-20\n\nstrain[1:5]\nTimeSeries{Float64}(5 samples, t0=1.12625664e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=0.001220703125s, name=\"strain\")\n\ncrop restricts a TimeSeries to a half-open GPS window [t_start, t_end), snapping each bound to the nearest sample — the natural way to zoom in on, say, the ten seconds around the GW150914 merger out of this 4096 s segment:\n\nmerger_gps = 1126259462.4\nzoom = crop(strain, merger_gps - 5, merger_gps + 5)\nTimeSeries{Float64}(40960 samples, t0=1.1262594573999023e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=10.0s, name=\"strain\")","title":"Indexing and cropping"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#Basic-arithmetic","page":"Getting Started","text":"+, -, *, and / all work elementwise. A TimeSeries combined with a plain number broadcasts as expected; two TimeSeries can only be combined when they share the exact same sample grid (same dt, t0, and length) — GravitationalWaveData.jl raises a specific ArgumentError naming which of the three checks failed rather than silently producing a meaningless result, see docs/CONVENTIONS.md.\n\ndoubled = zoom * 2\nTimeSeries{Float64}(40960 samples, t0=1.1262594573999023e9, dt=0.000244140625, duration=10.0s, name=\"strain\")\n\ndifference = doubled - zoom   # should reproduce `zoom` exactly\ndifference == zoom\nfalse","title":"Basic arithmetic"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/01_getting_started/#A-first-plot","page":"Getting Started","text":"Loading a RecipesBase-backed plotting package (here Plots.jl with the GR backend) enables a plot recipe for every GravitationalWaveData.jl series type directly — no extra glue code needed:\n\nusing Plots\ngr()\nPlots.GRBackend()\n\nplot(zoom; title=\"H1 strain around GW150914\", ylabel=\"Strain\")\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}\n\nThere's nothing visibly resembling a chirp signal here — raw strain is completely dominated by low-frequency detector noise. Pulling GW150914 out of that noise needs whitening and bandpassing first; see the Filtering & Whitening and Capstone tutorials for that.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"A first plot"}]
}
